{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cq8DwTaeSkpJaN4pQWr3gQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# A chatbot is not a tutor\n2\t\n3\tTalk source. 15 minutes. Audience: teaching colleagues and people who decide what\n4\tsoftware enters a course.\n5\t\n6\tFormat: one `## SLIDE` block per slide. The `<!--visual ... -->` comment holds the slide\n7\tHTML; everything else in the block is the narration, spoken in order. `build_deck.py`\n8\tturns this file into `deck.html` plus the narration file fed to `audioexplain`.\n9\t\n10\t**Provenance of every number on these slides.** The source is\n11\t`docs/sources/IA_generativa_aprendizaje_informe_completo.pdf`, which Kristian produced in\n12\ta ChatGPT conversation. Because it came from a language model, none of it went on a slide\n13\tuntil it was checked against primary sources:\n14\t\n15\t- Bastani et al., Tutor CoPilot, Eedi/LearnLM and the Khan A/B results were verified in\n16\t  task T-002 — see `coord/work/claude/T-002-carril-C-tutores-llm-juez.md`.\n17\t- The meta-analysis, the GenAI-vs-adaptive differential, Kestin, Nigeria, Sierra Leone,\n18\t  Mindspark, Robinson, Rori and ASSISTments were verified by `fable` in task T-006 — see\n19\t  `coord/work/fable/T-006-verificacion-cifras.md`, which carries the page-level URL for\n20\t  each one.\n21\t\n22\tThree of fable's findings are already applied here: Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD\n23\t(not 0.24, which is why the talk quotes only the 0.31 composite); Robinson's usage figure\n24\tis \"1 to 4 minutes\", not 4.4; and Sierra Leone's 0.380 ToT is not quoted at all, only the\n25\t0.258 ITT. Do not add a number to a slide without adding it to one of those two files\n26\tfirst.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## SLIDE 1 — Title\n31\t\n32\t<!--visual\n33\t<div class=\"title-slide\">\n34\t  <h1>A chatbot is not a tutor</h1>\n35\t  <p class=\"sub\">What the evidence says about AI and learning — and what to ask for\n36\t  before one enters your course</p>\n37\t  <p class=\"meta\">Evidence cutoff: August 2026</p>\n38\t</div>\n39\t-->\n40\t\n41\tI want to start with an experiment that should make all of us a little uncomfortable.\n42\t\n43\tThen I will give you the honest number for whether any of this works.\n44\t\n45\tAnd I will finish with the questions I would ask before letting one of these tools near\n46\tmy students.\n47\t\n48\t## SLIDE 1b — Where this comes from\n49\t\n50\t<!--visual\n51\t<h2>Where this talk comes from</h2>\n52\t<div class=\"pipeline\">\n53\t  <div class=\"step\"><b>1 · Draft</b><span>A research report I generated in a\n54\t    conversation with a language model</span></div>\n55\t  <div class=\"step\"><b>2 · Check</b><span>9 headline figures re-checked against the\n56\t    original papers &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>8 held, 1 was wrong</b></span></div>\n57\t  <div class=\"step\"><b>3 · Map</b><span>An earlier review of this literature from three\n58\t    angles: tutoring systems, interfaces and authoring, deployed LLM tutors</span></div>\n59\t  <div class=\"step\"><b>4 · This talk</b><span>Every slide labels which of the three it\n60\t    is showing you</span></div>\n61\t</div>\n62\t<p class=\"note\">Every claim on these slides carries one of four labels:</p>\n63\t<div class=\"eyebrow-row\">\n64\t  <span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· author, year</span></span>\n65\t  <span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on A + B</span></span>\n66\t  <span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our recommendation</span>\n67\t  <span class=\"prov prov-p\">Our project <span class=\"src\">· not yet evaluated</span></span>\n68\t</div>\n69\t<div class=\"provbar\"><b>Taxonomy:</b> <span class=\"chain\">designed by codex ·\n70\tprovenance audit of all slides in coord/work/codex/T-006-procedencia.md</span></div>\n71\t-->\n72\t\n73\tBefore any of the evidence, thirty seconds on where this talk comes from, because you\n74\tshould be able to tell my sources apart from my opinions.\n75\t\n76\tIt started as a research report I generated in a conversation with a language model.\n77\t\n78\tThat is a perfectly good way to start and a terrible way to finish, so nothing from it\n79\twent onto a slide until it had been checked against the original paper.\n80\t\n81\tNine headline figures were re-checked that way. Eight held.\n82\t\n83\tOne was wrong, and I will show you that one later, because correcting it turned out to\n84\tmake the argument better rather than weaker.\n85\t\n86\tUnderneath that sits an earlier review of this same literature from three separate\n87\tangles: the intelligent tutoring tradition, the interface and authoring side, and the\n88\tlanguage-model tutors that are actually deployed today.\n89\t\n90\tSo from here on, every claim on these slides carries one of four labels.\n91\t\n92\tEvidence, with the author and the year, means somebody measured it.\n93\t\n94\tOur synthesis means we built the conclusion by comparing sources. The citation proves the\n95\tinputs, not the conclusion.\n96\t\n97\tOur recommendation means it is a judgement about what you should do, and no experiment\n98\testablished it.\n99\t\n100\tAnd our project means it is a fact about the thing we are building, which is not the same\n101\tas a result.\n102\t\n103\tThe middle two are the ones you should argue with, and I have tried to make them easy to\n104\tfind rather than easy to miss.\n105\t\n106\t## SLIDE 1c — The question Bastani et al. asked\n107\t\n108\t<!--visual\n109\t<div class=\"card\">\n110\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the harm experiment</p>\n111\t  <h2>“How generative AI affects <em>learning</em> — how humans acquire new skills as\n112\t  they perform tasks.”</h2>\n113\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> three arms — an unrestricted assistant, a\n114\t  safeguarded tutor, and practice with no technology. The lecture and the final unassisted\n115\t  exam are identical across arms; only the practice tool changes.</p>\n116\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> a claim about AI harming learning in general.\n117\t  The safeguarded arm is the proof that the harm is not inevitable.</p>\n118\t  <p class=\"cite\">Bastani, H., Bastani, O., Sungu, A., Ge, H., Kabakcı, Ö., &amp; Mariman, R.\n119\t  (2025). <i>PNAS</i>, 122(26), e2422633122.</p>\n120\t</div>\n121\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the abstract</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Compiled in T-006 (fable) from the primary source</span></div>\n122\t-->\n123\t\n124\tBefore the result, the question they set out to answer, in their words: how generative AI\n125\taffects learning — how humans acquire new skills as they perform tasks.\n126\t\n127\tNotice that the question is about acquisition, not about performance. That is what makes\n128\tthe design work.\n129\t\n130\t## SLIDE 2 — The experiment\n131\t\n132\t<!--visual\n133\t<h2>One experiment, three groups</h2>\n134\t<div class=\"arms\">\n135\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">1</span><b>Unrestricted chat</b>\n136\t    <span>practice with a standard assistant</span></div>\n137\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">2</span><b>Tutored version</b>\n138\t    <span>same model, safeguards: hints, never the answer</span></div>\n139\t  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">3</span><b>Control</b>\n140\t    <span>practice with no technology</span></div>\n141\t</div>\n142\t<p class=\"note\">≈1,000 high-school mathematics students, randomly assigned.\n143\tPractice <b>with</b> the tool. Then the tool is removed and everyone sits the same exam.</p>\n144\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Bastani et al. 2025, PNAS</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → researched in T-002 (claude) → cited here</span></div>\n145\t-->\n146\t\n147\tNearly a thousand high school mathematics students were randomly assigned to three\n148\tgroups.\n149\t\n150\tThe first group practised with an ordinary chat assistant, with no restrictions.\n151\t\n152\tThe second group practised with the same underlying model, but wrapped in safeguards.\n153\tIt was built to give hints and withhold the answer.\n154\t\n155\tThe third group practised the way students have always practised, with no technology at\n156\tall.\n157\t\n158\tThen the researchers took the assistance away, and gave all three groups the same exam.\n159\t\n160\tHold on to that last step, because it is the entire design. The measurement happens\n161\tafter the help is gone.\n162\t\n163\t## SLIDE 3 — The result\n164\t\n165\t<!--visual\n166\t<h2>What happened</h2>\n167\t<table class=\"results\">\n168\t  <tr><th></th><th>During practice<br><span>with the tool</span></th>\n169\t      <th>On the exam<br><span>without it</span></th></tr>\n170\t  <tr><td><b>Unrestricted chat</b></td>\n171\t      <td class=\"up\">+48%</td><td class=\"down\">−17%</td></tr>\n172\t  <tr><td><b>Tutored, with safeguards</b></td>\n173\t      <td class=\"up\">+127%</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ control</td></tr>\n174\t</table>\n175\t<p class=\"note\">Both relative to the control group. Safeguards removed the harm.\n176\tThey did not produce an advantage.</p>\n177\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Bastani et al. 2025, PNAS</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → verified in T-002 (claude)</span></div>\n178\t-->\n179\t\n180\tDuring the practice sessions, the unrestricted group performed forty-eight per cent\n181\tbetter than the control group.\n182\t\n183\tOn the exam, without the tool, the same students performed seventeen per cent worse than\n184\tthe control group.\n185\t\n186\tNow look at the tutored version, the one built carefully, with safeguards.\n187\t\n188\tDuring practice it did even better. A hundred and twenty-seven per cent above control.\n189\t\n190\tAnd on the exam it came out level with the control group. No better, no worse.\n191\t\n192\tSo the careful design succeeded at something real. It removed the damage. What it did\n193\tnot do was produce any measurable learning advantage of its own.\n194\t\n195\t## SLIDE 4 — The lesson\n196\t\n197\t<!--visual\n198\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n199\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The lesson</p>\n200\t  <h2>Performance <em>with</em> the tool is not learning.</h2>\n201\t  <p class=\"sub\">The number that looked most like success was the number that predicted\n202\t  it least.</p>\n203\t</div>\n204\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Bastani + the ITS literature</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">No paper states this in these words; the inference is mine</span></div>\n205\t-->\n206\t\n207\tI want to be careful about what this does and does not show.\n208\t\n209\tIt is not evidence that artificial intelligence damages learning in general. The second\n210\tgroup is the proof that it does not have to.\n211\t\n212\tIt is evidence about measurement, and that makes it more useful, not less.\n213\t\n214\tThe moment that looked most like success — a student solving more problems, faster, with\n215\thelp available — was the moment that told us least about whether anything had been\n216\tlearned.\n217\t\n218\tAnd here is the practical consequence for anyone choosing software. If you evaluate a\n219\ttool by watching students use it, that can lead you to prefer the tools that do the most\n220\twork for them.\n221\t\n222\tI am not claiming that is what buyers systematically do — nobody has measured that. I am\n223\tclaiming this experiment shows the metric is capable of inverting the ranking.\n224\t\n225\tWhich is enough reason to be a little suspicious of a demonstration that feels\n226\twonderful.\n227\t\n228\t## SLIDE 4b — The question the meta-analysis asked\n229\t\n230\t<!--visual\n231\t<div class=\"card\">\n232\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the meta-analysis</p>\n233\t  <h2>“Adaptive educational technology promises the personalization of teaching at a\n234\t  fraction of its cost, but experimental estimates vary widely in magnitude and in sign,\n235\t  and existing syntheses <em>predate generative AI</em>.”</h2>\n236\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>What they did:</b> brought computer-assisted learning platforms and\n237\t  generative AI tools into a common framework, under common inclusion criteria, on a common\n238\t  effect-size scale.</p>\n239\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> treating the subgroups as settled. The authors\n240\t  call them suggestive rather than conclusive, and no included study is from a low-income\n241\t  country.</p>\n242\t  <p class=\"cite\">Burneo, A., Dinarte-Diaz, L., Lopez, C., &amp; Molina, E. (2026). Background\n243\t  paper, <i>World Development Report 2026</i>, World Bank. Working paper.</p>\n244\t</div>\n245\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the abstract</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the primary PDF</span></div>\n246\t-->\n247\t\n248\tAnd the same for the meta-analysis, because its question tells you what it can settle.\n249\t\n250\tThey note that experimental estimates vary widely in magnitude and even in sign, and that\n251\tthe existing syntheses all predate generative AI.\n252\t\n253\tSo what they built is a common frame: old-style adaptive platforms and new generative\n254\ttools, under the same inclusion criteria, on the same effect-size scale.\n255\t\n256\tThat is what makes the next two slides possible, and it is also the reason they can\n257\tcompare the generations at all.\n258\t\n259\t## SLIDE 5 — So does it work?\n260\t\n261\t<!--visual\n262\t<h2>So does it work?</h2>\n263\t<div class=\"stat-row\">\n264\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.125</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n265\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">average effect on learning<br>vs traditional instruction</span></div>\n266\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">14</span>\n267\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">randomised trials<br>191 effect sizes, 10 economies</span></div>\n268\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.12</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n269\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">the AI tutoring<br>and instruction subset</span></div>\n270\t</div>\n271\t<p class=\"note\">A real effect. Above the median for field interventions in education.\n272\tNot a revolution. <b>Read the base honestly:</b> a World Bank working paper, not yet\n273\tpeer-reviewed; 14 studies; no included study from a low-income country.</p>\n274\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo et al. 2026, World Bank</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Working paper, not peer-reviewed → verified in T-006 (fable) · the “not a revolution” judgement is mine</span></div>\n275\t-->\n276\t\n277\tSo, does any of it work? Yes, on average, modestly, and the honest number is not\n278\tthrilling.\n279\t\n280\tThe most recent meta-analysis pools a hundred and ninety-one effect estimates from\n281\tfourteen randomised trials across ten economies.\n282\t\n283\tThe average effect on learning is about an eighth of a standard deviation. Plus zero\n284\tpoint one two five.\n285\t\n286\tThe subset that is specifically artificial intelligence tutoring or instruction sits at\n287\troughly the same value.\n288\t\n289\tThat is a genuine effect, and it sits above the median for education interventions\n290\tmeasured in real classrooms. It is worth having.\n291\t\n292\tIt is also not a transformation of education, and anyone selling you one is running\n293\tahead of the evidence.\n294\t\n295\tAnd since I am going to spend the rest of this talk being demanding, let me be fair for\n296\ta moment about what this technology genuinely does well.\n297\t\n298\tIt is available at eleven at night, which no tutor of mine ever was.\n299\t\n300\tIt is endlessly patient, and it does not sigh when a student asks the same question for\n301\tthe third time.\n302\t\n303\tIt will explain the same idea in four different ways, in the student's own language, at\n304\twhatever reading level they need.\n305\t\n306\tThose are real gains in access, and for some students access is the whole problem.\n307\t\n308\tMy argument is not that this is worthless. It is that access and availability are not the\n309\tsame thing as instruction, and the evidence only rewards us when we build the second one\n310\ton top of the first.\n311\t\n312\t## SLIDE 6 — The number nobody quotes\n313\t\n314\t<!--visual\n315\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n316\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The number that never appears in a pitch</p>\n317\t  <h2>Generative AI vs the previous generation of adaptive tutors</h2>\n318\t  <div class=\"stat-inline\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0.022</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n319\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">estimated difference &nbsp;·&nbsp; standard error <b>0.075</b>\n320\t    &nbsp;·&nbsp; CI [−0.15, 0.19]</span></div>\n321\t  <p class=\"sub\">Not evidence of equivalence. Absence of a demonstrated advantage.</p>\n322\t  <p class=\"note\">The authors' own words: the interval <em>“bounds the difference between\n323\t  generations rather than resolving it”</em> — and “the experimental record to date shows\n324\t  no advantage for the newer technology.”</p>\n325\t</div>\n326\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo et al. 2026 — authors’ own wording</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → verified in T-006 (fable) · “still the benchmark” is my reading</span></div>\n327\t-->\n328\t\n329\tHere is the number that almost never appears in a product presentation.\n330\t\n331\tWhen you compare generative systems against the previous generation of adaptive tutoring\n332\tsoftware — the kind that existed well before large language models — the estimated\n333\tdifference is about two hundredths of a standard deviation.\n334\t\n335\tThe standard error on that estimate is more than three times the estimate itself.\n336\t\n337\tI want to state that carefully, because it is easy to overclaim in either direction.\n338\t\n339\tThis is not proof that the two are equivalent. The evidence base is simply not yet able\n340\tto rank the generations.\n341\t\n342\tBut it does mean something concrete for a purchasing decision. The older, less\n343\tconversational, less impressive-looking technology is still the benchmark, and it has not\n344\tbeen beaten.\n345\t\n346\t## SLIDE 7 — The average hides the lesson\n347\t\n348\t<!--visual\n349\t<h2>The average is the least interesting part</h2>\n350\t<table class=\"results wide\">\n351\t  <tr><th>Study</th><th>Effect</th><th>What was actually deployed</th></tr>\n352\t  <tr><td>University physics, crossover</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.63 SD</td>\n353\t      <td>expert-written lessons, verified solutions, sequence enforced by the platform</td></tr>\n354\t  <tr><td>Nigeria, after-school</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.31 SD</td>\n355\t      <td>AI + extra time + teachers present + students working in pairs</td></tr>\n356\t  <tr><td>Sierra Leone, school-scale</td><td class=\"up\">+0.258 SD</td>\n357\t      <td>teacher-led classes; teachers set the objectives</td></tr>\n358\t  <tr><td>AI reading tutor + a human<br>to drive engagement</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ 0</td>\n359\t      <td><b>both arms had the platform</b>; what was randomised was adding a person whose\n360\t      job was engagement, not teaching</td></tr>\n361\t</table>\n362\t<p class=\"note\">Read each honestly: the physics result is two lessons with an immediate\n363\ttest at one elite university; Nigeria lost 569 of 1,328 students before the final test;\n364\tSierra Leone is a provider report, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n365\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Kestin 2025 · De Simone 2025 · LearnLM/Fab AI 2026 · Robinson 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">All four verified in T-006 (fable) · Sierra Leone is a provider report</span></div>\n366\t-->\n367\t\n368\tThe average is the least interesting part of that meta-analysis, because the spread\n369\taround it is enormous.\n370\t\n371\tA crossover study in university physics found about six tenths of a standard deviation.\n372\t\n373\tAn after-school programme in Nigeria found about three tenths.\n374\t\n375\tA school-scale deployment in Sierra Leone found a quarter of a standard deviation.\n376\t\n377\tAnd a pair of trials on an AI reading tutor found essentially nothing on reading.\n378\t\n379\tThat last row needs a caveat, and I would rather give it to you than have you find it\n380\tafterwards. In those trials both groups already had the platform. What was randomised was\n381\tadding a human being whose job was to keep students engaged with it. So the null is about\n382\tthe human support, not about access itself.\n383\t\n384\tSame broad technology. Completely different outcomes. So the interesting question is not\n385\twhether AI works. It is what distinguishes the top row from the bottom one.\n386\t\n387\t## SLIDE 7b — What each of those three actually asked\n388\t\n389\t<!--visual\n390\t<h2>Three studies, three different questions</h2>\n391\t<div class=\"qgrid\">\n392\t  <div><b>Kestin et al. — physics</b>\n393\t    <span>“Measure the difference between how much students learn … when <em>identical\n394\t    material</em> is presented through an AI tutor compared with an active learning\n395\t    classroom.”<br><br>Not AI versus nothing. Not AI versus a lecture. AI versus the best\n396\t    thing we already do.</span></div>\n397\t  <div><b>De Simone et al. — Nigeria</b>\n398\t    <span>“Whether generative AI … can help solve that problem” — large learning deficits\n399\t    with scarce teaching resources.<br><br>The control “did not receive any intervention”.\n400\t    So +0.31 SD is the effect of the whole after-school package, not of the model.</span></div>\n401\t  <div><b>LearnLM/Fab AI — Sierra Leone</b>\n402\t    <span>“How does the <em>teacher-led integration</em> of Guided Learning into mathematics\n403\t    classes … affect student learning outcomes?”<br><br>Same teacher training in both arms.\n404\t    The question is about integration, not about the app.</span></div>\n405\t</div>\n406\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· questions quoted from each paper</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Compiled and checked in T-006 (fable) · Sierra Leone is a provider report</span></div>\n407\t-->\n408\t\n409\tIt is worth putting the three questions side by side, because they are not the same\n410\tquestion and the effect sizes are not comparable in the way a table makes them look.\n411\t\n412\tKestin asked whether an AI tutor beats an active learning classroom when the material is\n413\tidentical. Not AI against nothing, and not AI against a lecture — AI against the best\n414\tthing we already know how to do. That is the hardest comparison in the set, and it is why\n415\tthat number is striking.\n416\t\n417\tNigeria asked whether generative AI can help with large learning deficits and scarce\n418\tteaching resources. Their control group received no intervention at all. So the three\n419\ttenths is the effect of the entire after-school package, not of the model inside it.\n420\t\n421\tSierra Leone asked something different again: how does teacher-led integration of the tool\n422\tinto maths classes affect outcomes. Both arms had the same teacher training. The question\n423\tthere is about integration, not about the app.\n424\t\n425\tThree questions, three contrasts, one table. That is exactly how a literature gets\n426\tmisread.\n427\t\n428\t## SLIDE 8 — Effects are recipes\n429\t\n430\t<!--visual\n431\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n432\t  <h2>Across these cases, the large effects look like <em>recipes</em>,\n433\t  not properties of the model.</h2>\n434\t  <ul class=\"clean\">\n435\t    <li>Content written and checked by subject experts</li>\n436\t    <li>A sequence the platform enforces from <b>outside</b> the model</li>\n437\t    <li>Extra time that was actually scheduled</li>\n438\t    <li>A teacher or facilitator in the room</li>\n439\t  </ul>\n440\t  <p class=\"sub\">Copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment.\n441\t  Neither does copying the prompt.</p>\n442\t</div>\n443\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Kestin + Nigeria + Sierra Leone</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">The ingredients are measured; that they form a necessary recipe is not</span></div>\n444\t-->\n445\t\n446\tLook at what was actually deployed in these four cases, and a pattern appears.\n447\t\n448\tThe physics result used lessons designed by subject experts, with worked solutions\n449\tprepared in advance, and a sequence the platform imposed from outside the model.\n450\t\n451\tThat detail deserves a moment. The researchers reported that the prompt alone could not\n452\thold the teaching sequence reliably, so they moved the sequence out of the prompt and\n453\tinto the software around it.\n454\t\n455\tThe Nigerian programme was after-school sessions, with additional instructional time,\n456\tteachers present, and students working in pairs.\n457\t\n458\tSierra Leone was ordinary classes, led by teachers who set the objectives.\n459\t\n460\tAcross these high-effect cases, what was tested is a package: verified content, an\n461\tenforced sequence, protected time, and people.\n462\t\n463\tI want to be careful here, because this is my reading and not a finding. Nobody has\n464\ttested whether those four ingredients are each necessary, and Sierra Leone does not\n465\tcontain all of them.\n466\t\n467\tWhat I am confident saying is the negative version. Copying the brand does not reproduce\n468\tthe treatment. And copying the prompt does not reproduce it either.\n469\t\n470\t## SLIDE 8b — The question Robinson et al. asked\n471\t\n472\t<!--visual\n473\t<div class=\"card\">\n474\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the take-up null</p>\n475\t  <h2>“An engaged, in-person human tutor whose role is to support student engagement with\n476\t  an AI literacy tutor, <em>not to deliver direct instruction</em>.”</h2>\n477\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> students used the AI literacy platform\n478\t  independently, or with that in-person tutor. <b>Both arms had the platform.</b></p>\n479\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> “access to an AI tutor produced zero learning”\n480\t  as an experimental result. There is no no-AI arm, so this design cannot estimate access\n481\t  against nothing.</p>\n482\t  <p class=\"cite\">Robinson, C. D., Gormley, D., Trindade Ribeiro, A., &amp; Loeb, S. (2026).\n483\t  EdWorkingPaper 26-1451, Annenberg Institute, Brown University.</p>\n484\t</div>\n485\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the introduction</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">This card exists because fable caught the talk misreading the contrast (T-006)</span></div>\n486\t-->\n487\t\n488\tAnd this one I want to give you carefully, because an earlier version of this talk got it\n489\twrong, and it is the kind of wrong that gets found in the question session rather than in\n490\tthe preparation.\n491\t\n492\tWhat they tested was an in-person tutor whose role was to support engagement with an AI\n493\tliteracy tutor — explicitly not to deliver instruction.\n494\t\n495\tBoth groups had the platform. What was randomised was the human being.\n496\t\n497\tSo this study cannot tell you what happens when you compare access against nothing,\n498\tbecause there is no arm without the AI.\n499\t\n500\t## SLIDE 9 — The null result that matters most\n501\t\n502\t<!--visual\n503\t<h2>The metric that moved, and the one that didn't</h2>\n504\t<p class=\"lede\">Two RCTs, ≈350 primary students. <b>Both arms had the AI tutor.</b> What\n505\twas randomised: adding an in-person tutor whose role was engagement, not instruction.</p>\n506\t<div class=\"stat-row\">\n507\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+71–80%</span>\n508\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">engagement<br><b>the number that goes in the deck</b></span></div>\n509\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+1 to 4</span>\n510\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">minutes of actual use<br>per week &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>the real dose</b></span></div>\n511\t  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0</span>\n512\t    <span class=\"stat-l\">improvement<br>in reading</span></div>\n513\t</div>\n514\t<p class=\"note\">Descriptive, not randomised: in the access-only arm nearly half never\n515\topened the platform, and those who did averaged 2–5 minutes a week.\n516\t<b>A percentage is not minutes.</b></p>\n517\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Robinson et al. 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Correction trail: source report said 4.4 min → primary abstract says 1–4 → corrected after verification in T-006 (fable)</span></div>\n518\t-->\n519\t\n520\tThe most useful result in this area is a null one, and it is the least discussed.\n521\t\n522\tTwo randomised trials, around three hundred and fifty primary school children. Both\n523\tgroups had the AI reading tutor. The thing being tested was adding a person whose job was\n524\tto keep them using it.\n525\t\n526\tEngagement rose by between seventy-one and eighty per cent.\n527\t\n528\tThat is the number that goes in the deck. Now the same result in absolute terms.\n529\t\n530\tAverage use went up by one to four minutes per week.\n531\t\n532\tAnd reading did not improve at all.\n533\t\n534\tI find this the most clarifying pair of numbers in the whole literature, because both are\n535\ttrue and they tell opposite stories.\n536\t\n537\tA relative rise in engagement of eighty per cent sounds like adoption. One to four\n538\tminutes a week is not a dose of anything.\n539\t\n540\tThere is one more number here, and I want to label it carefully because it is descriptive\n541\trather than randomised. In the arm that had the platform and no human support, nearly half\n542\tthe students never opened it at all, and those who did averaged two to five minutes a\n543\tweek.\n544\t\n545\tSo keep this one in your pocket for the next time someone proposes buying licences for\n546\teverybody. Take-up is not an implementation detail. It is part of the treatment, and it\n547\thas to be planned and measured like one.\n548\t\n549\t## SLIDE 10 — What is under the conversation\n550\t\n551\t<!--visual\n552\t<h2>The conversation is the surface</h2>\n553\t<div class=\"stack\">\n554\t  <div class=\"layer surface\">Conversation and interface</div>\n555\t  <div class=\"layer\">Guardrails — attempt before hint, no answer on demand, fading</div>\n556\t  <div class=\"layer\">Tools and verification — calculation and facts <b>outside</b> the model</div>\n557\t  <div class=\"layer\">Pedagogical policy — what to do next, and why</div>\n558\t  <div class=\"layer\">Learner model — a structured estimate, not a chat history</div>\n559\t  <div class=\"layer\">Domain model — concepts, prerequisites, worked solutions</div>\n560\t  <div class=\"layer base\">Human orchestration and independent measurement</div>\n561\t</div>\n562\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· design framework, condensed from the source report §3</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">A recommended architecture, not a measured effect</span></div>\n563\t-->\n564\t\n565\tIf the effects come from packages, it is worth asking what a package actually contains.\n566\t\n567\tThe conversation is the surface. It is the part you see in a demonstration, and usually\n568\tthe only part a demonstration shows.\n569\t\n570\tUnderneath, a serious system needs a map of the subject, with prerequisites and worked\n571\tsolutions.\n572\t\n573\tIt needs a structured estimate of what this particular student knows. Not a\n574\tconversation history — an actual state, that can be inspected.\n575\t\n576\tThat distinction is worth making concrete, because it is where most products quietly\n577\tfail.\n578\t\n579\tA chat history says: this student asked about the budget constraint twice and seemed\n580\tconfused.\n581\t\n582\tA learner model, in the system we are building, could instead say: this student can\n583\tcompute the slope, cannot interpret it as an opportunity cost, has not demonstrated the\n584\tfeasible set at all, and the last two pieces of evidence came with hints, so they do not\n585\tcount.\n586\t\n587\tThe first is a transcript. The second is something a teacher can read, disagree with, and\n588\tcorrect.\n589\t\n590\tIt needs an explicit policy for what to do next, and a reason for choosing it.\n591\t\n592\tIt needs tools that calculate and verify, sitting outside the model that does the\n593\ttalking.\n594\t\n595\tIt needs limits on what it is willing to reveal, and support that withdraws as the\n596\tstudent improves.\n597\t\n598\tAnd it needs a teacher who can see what it believes and overrule it, plus a way of\n599\tmeasuring itself that is not its own opinion.\n600\t\n601\t## SLIDE 11 — Four levels of personalization\n602\t\n603\t<!--visual\n604\t<h2>Four levels of \"personalized\"</h2>\n605\t<ol class=\"levels\">\n606\t  <li><b>Presentation</b> — tone, language, length, readability\n607\t      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n608\t  <li><b>Interaction</b> — the example, the hint, the next question in this turn\n609\t      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n610\t  <li><b>Pedagogical decision</b> — the next action, chosen from a diagnosis\n611\t      <span class=\"tag rare\">evidence thin</span></li>\n612\t  <li><b>Trajectory</b> — across sessions: prerequisites, forgetting, transfer\n613\t      <span class=\"tag rare\">evidence thin</span></li>\n614\t</ol>\n615\t<p class=\"note big\">Remembering your name is not the same as estimating what you know.</p>\n616\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our framework <span class=\"src\">· our taxonomy, not a validated one</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Public causal evidence does not isolate levels 3–4; the split is ours</span></div>\n617\t-->\n618\t\n619\tThe word personalized is doing a great deal of unearned work in this field, so it helps\n620\tto break it into four levels.\n621\t\n622\tLevel one changes presentation: tone, language, length, reading level.\n623\t\n624\tLevel two changes the interaction: which example, which hint, which question comes next\n625\tin this turn.\n626\t\n627\tMany of the products you will be shown stop there, and both levels are genuinely useful\n628\tfor access.\n629\t\n630\tLevel three chooses the next action based on a diagnosis of what this student got wrong,\n631\tand why.\n632\t\n633\tLevel four maintains a trajectory across sessions, tracking prerequisites, forgetting and\n634\ttransfer.\n635\t\n636\tThree and four are what this framework treats as genuine pedagogical adaptation. I should\n637\tbe careful about how I put the next part: the public causal evidence does not isolate\n638\tthose levels and tell us the effects live there. It is thinner than that.\n639\t\n640\tThe test I find most useful is this. A system that remembers your name, adopts a warm\n641\ttone, and rephrases the same content can feel deeply personal while estimating nothing at\n642\tall about what you know.\n643\t\n644\t## SLIDE 12 — What to ask for\n645\t\n646\t<!--visual\n647\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n648\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The one question that separates a system from a demo</p>\n649\t  <h2>\"Show me the learner model before and after three answers.<br>\n650\t  What changed, and why?\"</h2>\n651\t</div>\n652\t<ol class=\"checks\">\n653\t  <li>Curriculum traceable to sources you authorised</li>\n654\t  <li>A student state the teacher can see — and correct when it is wrong</li>\n655\t  <li>An explicit help policy: attempt before hint, no answer on demand, support that fades</li>\n656\t  <li>Calculation and fact-checking done by tools, outside the language model</li>\n657\t  <li>Evidence on your population, on your outcome, measured without the tool present</li>\n658\t</ol>\n659\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our recommendation <span class=\"src\">· my 5-point shortlist, condensed from the report’s 10 requirements</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Normative. No experiment established this list</span></div>\n660\t-->\n661\t\n662\tSo here is what I would ask for, and it fits on a single slide.\n663\t\n664\tStart with one question, because it separates a real system from a good demonstration.\n665\t\n666\tShow me the student model before and after three answers. Tell me what changed, and why\n667\tit changed.\n668\t\n669\tIf the answer is a longer chat history, you are looking at level two dressed as level\n670\tfour.\n671\t\n672\tThen five checks.\n673\t\n674\tIs the curriculum traceable to sources you authorised, rather than to the open web.\n675\t\n676\tCan a teacher see the estimated state, and correct it when it is wrong.\n677\t\n678\tIs there an explicit help policy — an attempt required before a hint, no answer on\n679\tdemand, and support that fades as the student improves.\n680\t\n681\tAre calculation and fact-checking done by tools outside the language model, rather than\n682\tby the model marking its own arithmetic.\n683\t\n684\tAnd is there evidence on a population like yours, on an outcome like yours, measured\n685\twithout the tool in the room.\n686\t\n687\tIf you only have two minutes with a vendor, there is a faster probe. Sit down in front of\n688\tthe system and ask it, plainly, to just give you the answer.\n689\t\n690\tThen ask again, and be a little rude about it.\n691\t\n692\tWhat you are testing is whether the refusal is a policy or a personality. A policy holds\n693\ton the third request. A personality folds, and every one of your students will find that\n694\tout before the end of the first week.\n695\t\n696\t## SLIDE 13 — How to measure\n697\t\n698\t<!--visual\n699\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n700\t  <p class=\"kicker\">The measurement rule</p>\n701\t  <h2>Independent test. No AI present. Ideally after a delay.</h2>\n702\t  <p class=\"sub\">Everything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems per hour — measures\n703\t  productivity. Worth tracking. Not the outcome.</p>\n704\t  <p class=\"note big\">And count real minutes of use. A licence is not a dose.</p>\n705\t</div>\n706\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our measurement rule <span class=\"src\">· informed by Bastani + the evaluation framework</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">A standard we recommend, not an estimate</span></div>\n707\t-->\n708\t\n709\tAnd on that last point, the measurement rule is short enough to remember on the way out.\n710\t\n711\tThe primary outcome should be an independent test, taken without the system present, and\n712\tideally after a delay of days or weeks.\n713\t\n714\tEverything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems completed per hour, next-question\n715\taccuracy — measures productivity while assisted.\n716\t\n717\tThose are worth tracking. I track them. They are simply not the outcome, and the first\n718\texperiment I showed you is what happens when they are treated as one.\n719\t\n720\tAnd count real minutes of use, by student. Not licences issued, not accounts created.\n721\t\n722\t## SLIDE 13b — Three questions nobody has answered\n723\t\n724\t<!--visual\n725\t<h2>Three questions nobody has answered</h2>\n726\t<p class=\"lede\">Not a wish-list. Each one is unanswered, would change what you do next\n727\tsemester, and has a reason it has not been asked.</p>\n728\t<ol class=\"questions\">\n729\t  <li><b>Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?</b>\n730\t    <span>No trial has an active control: same extra time, same structure, same adult\n731\t    supervision, without the AI.</span></li>\n732\t  <li><b>Generative or the previous generation — which, for the same content and time?</b>\n733\t    <span>The head-to-head trial does not exist. Our headline 0.022 is a cross-study\n734\t    comparison, not a race.</span></li>\n735\t  <li><b>Is take-up itself the treatment?</b>\n736\t    <span>Nobody has held the tool constant and randomised how it enters the classroom.</span></li>\n737\t</ol>\n738\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· gaps identified in T-006 (fable)</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Each gap checked against the studies in the record</span></div>\n739\t-->\n740\t\n741\tI want to end the evidence part with the three questions that nobody has answered, because\n742\tfor this audience they are more useful than the answers we do have.\n743\t\n744\tEach one had to clear a bar: no study in the record answers it, an answer would change\n745\twhat you do next semester, and I can tell you why it has not been asked.\n746\t\n747\t## SLIDE 13c — Question one: the missing control\n748\t\n749\t<!--visual\n750\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n751\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Question one</p>\n752\t  <h2>Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?</h2>\n753\t</div>\n754\t<div class=\"qgrid\">\n755\t  <div><b>The gap</b><span>Every large positive effect is a bundle — AI <em>plus</em> extra\n756\t    scheduled time, structure and adult attention — measured against a control that got no\n757\t    extra anything. Nigeria's control received no intervention; treatment got 18 extra hours\n758\t    of supervised sessions. Rori added two 30-minute sessions on top of normal class.</span></div>\n759\t  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>If most of the gain is the scheduled, supervised\n760\t    practice block, you can run that block without buying anything. If it is the AI, the\n761\t    licence is the cheapest ingredient. Opposite budget decisions.</span></div>\n762\t  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>An active control roughly doubles cost and answers a\n763\t    question neither vendors nor implementing researchers are motivated to ask — in the\n764\t    meta-analysis, researchers implemented 16 of 19 interventions.</span></div>\n765\t</div>\n766\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· De Simone 2025 · Henkel 2024 · Burneo 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Designs are from the papers; that the gap matters for your budget is my inference</span></div>\n767\t-->\n768\t\n769\tQuestion one. Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?\n770\t\n771\tEvery large positive effect in this literature is a bundle. The AI, plus extra scheduled\n772\ttime, plus structure, plus adult attention — measured against a control group that got no\n773\textra anything.\n774\t\n775\tIn Nigeria the control received no intervention at all, while the treatment group got\n776\teighteen extra hours of supervised sessions. In Ghana it was two extra half-hour sessions\n777\ta week on top of normal class.\n778\t\n779\tSo nobody has run the arm that would settle it: same extra time, same structure, same\n780\tadult supervision, and no AI.\n781\t\n782\tAnd notice why this matters for your budget. If most of that third of a standard deviation\n783\tcomes from the scheduled, supervised practice block, then you can run that block without\n784\tbuying anything at all. If it comes from the software, the licence is the cheapest\n785\tingredient in the recipe.\n786\t\n787\tThose are opposite decisions, and today the evidence cannot separate them.\n788\t\n789\tWhy has nobody done it? An active control roughly doubles the cost of the study, and it\n790\tanswers a question that neither the vendors nor the implementing researchers are\n791\tespecially motivated to ask. In the meta-analysis I showed you, the researchers themselves\n792\timplemented sixteen of the nineteen interventions.\n793\t\n794\t## SLIDE 13d — Question two: the race that was never run\n795\t\n796\t<!--visual\n797\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n798\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Question two</p>\n799\t  <h2>Generative, or the previous generation — for the same content, in the same time?</h2>\n800\t</div>\n801\t<div class=\"qgrid\">\n802\t  <div><b>The gap</b><span>No study randomises students between a pre-LLM adaptive platform\n803\t    and a generative tutor teaching the same curriculum in the same time. The 0.022 from\n804\t    earlier is a <em>cross-study</em> estimate: different trials, populations, subjects and\n805\t    dosages.</span></div>\n806\t  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>This is the actual procurement decision. The\n807\t    auditable, cheaper, non-conversational lineage benchmarks at +0.18 to +0.37 SD. Today\n808\t    the choice between them is made on demos.</span></div>\n809\t  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>It needs two production-grade systems on identical\n810\t    content, and it settles a comparison no vendor wants to lose. A meta-analysis can only\n811\t    synthesise the experiments that exist.</span></div>\n812\t</div>\n813\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo 2026 · Muralidharan 2019 · Roschelle 2016</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">The absence of a head-to-head trial was established in T-006 (fable)</span></div>\n814\t-->\n815\t\n816\tQuestion two. Generative, or the previous generation, for the same content in the same\n817\ttime?\n818\t\n819\tI showed you zero point zero two two earlier, and I owe you a clarification about what\n820\tkind of number that is. It is a cross-study comparison — different trials, different\n821\tpopulations, different subjects, different dosages — not a race between two systems.\n822\t\n823\tThe race has never been run. No study randomises students between a pre-language-model\n824\tadaptive platform and a generative tutor teaching the same curriculum in the same amount\n825\tof time.\n826\t\n827\tAnd this is the actual purchasing decision on the table. The older, auditable, cheaper,\n828\tnon-conversational lineage benchmarks between about zero point one eight and zero point\n829\tthree seven of a standard deviation. That is not a weak competitor.\n830\t\n831\tSo today the choice between the two generations gets made on the strength of a\n832\tdemonstration, because the head-to-head number does not exist.\n833\t\n834\tIt is unanswered because it needs two production-grade systems built on identical content,\n835\twhich is expensive, and because it settles a comparison that no vendor wants to lose.\n836\t\n837\t## SLIDE 13e — Question three: is take-up the treatment?\n838\t\n839\t<!--visual\n840\t<div class=\"big-idea\">\n841\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Question three</p>\n842\t  <h2>Is take-up itself the treatment?</h2>\n843\t</div>\n844\t<div class=\"qgrid\">\n845\t  <div><b>The gap</b><span>Integration mode tracks outcomes almost perfectly — mandated,\n846\t    teacher-led, scheduled use gives +0.26 to +0.31 SD; optional access gives near-zero use.\n847\t    But that comparison runs across different tools, subjects and countries, so integration\n848\t    is confounded with everything.</span></div>\n849\t  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>It answers whether putting a link in the syllabus is\n850\t    an intervention or a placebo — and whether the scarce resource to plan for is the tool\n851\t    or the protected time in which students must use it.</span></div>\n852\t  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>The field logs usage as an implementation covariate\n853\t    instead of designing it as the treatment. And randomising timetable policy inside one\n854\t    school raises fairness objections that after-school add-ons quietly avoid.</span></div>\n855\t</div>\n856\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Sierra Leone + Nigeria + Robinson</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Integration mode is confounded across studies — that is the point, not a finding</span></div>\n857\t-->\n858\t\n859\tQuestion three, and it is the one I would most like someone in this room to answer. Is\n860\ttake-up itself the treatment?\n861\t\n862\tAcross these studies, how the tool enters the classroom tracks the outcome almost\n863\tperfectly. Mandated, teacher-led, scheduled use gives you a quarter to a third of a\n864\tstandard deviation. Optional access gives you almost no use at all.\n865\t\n866\tBut that comparison runs across different tools, different subjects and different\n867\tcountries, so the integration mode is confounded with everything else.\n868\t\n869\tNobody has held the tool constant and randomised the policy: scheduled use inside class,\n870\tagainst recommended optional access.\n871\t\n872\tFor a department, that is the question with money attached. It tells you whether putting a\n873\tlink in the syllabus is a real intervention or a placebo, and whether the scarce resource\n874\tyou should be protecting is not the software at all, but the timetabled hour in which\n875\tstudents are actually required to use it.\n876\t\n877\tIt is unanswered partly through a blind spot — the field records usage as an\n878\timplementation detail rather than designing it as the treatment — and partly because\n879\trandomising timetable policy inside one school raises fairness objections that an\n880\tafter-school add-on quietly avoids.\n881\t\n882\tAnd there is a fourth question I will mention only in passing, because it is the one that\n883\tshould embarrass all of us. The proposition that motivates this entire literature is that\n884\tpersonalisation can be delivered at a fraction of the cost of human tutoring. Only two of\n885\tthe fourteen studies report per-student costs on a comparable basis. Human tutoring pools\n886\tat around zero point two nine of a standard deviation, against zero point one two for the\n887\tAI subgroup. Whether AI wins per dollar — which is the only frame in which a department\n888\tactually decides — has essentially never been measured next to the effects it is meant to\n889\tjustify.\n890\t\n891\t## SLIDE 14 — Applying it to our own work\n892\t\n893\t<!--visual\n894\t<h2>What this changed in our own project</h2>\n895\t<p class=\"lede\"><b>tutorIA</b> — a small interactive tutor for intermediate\n896\tmicroeconomics: narrated explanation, a manipulable budget line, ungraded questions,\n897\tand a judge that diagnoses misconceptions by name.</p>\n898\t<ol class=\"changes\">\n899\t  <li><b>The tutor interrupts at designed points</b>, instead of waiting at an open chat\n900\t  window — the largest deployed AI tutor reports most students simply never used the\n901\t  chat.</li>\n902\t  <li><b>Evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery</b> — a hint\n903\t  invalidates the attempt, and we re-test with a fresh item.</li>\n904\t  <li><b>An unassisted check at the end of each concept</b> — no grade, never shown to\n905\t  the student as an assessment, feeding only the instructor's diagnosis.</li>\n906\t</ol>\n907\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-p\">Our project <span class=\"src\">· evidence-informed decisions, not results</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">tutorIA has not been evaluated. No trial has been run</span></div>\n908\t-->\n909\t\n910\tLet me close by applying this to my own work, because it is easy to give this talk and\n911\tthen not follow it.\n912\t\n913\tWe are building a small interactive tutor for intermediate microeconomics. Narrated\n914\texplanation, a budget line the student can drag, ungraded questions, and a component that\n915\tdiagnoses misconceptions by name.\n916\t\n917\tReading this evidence changed three decisions.\n918\t\n919\tThe first: the tutor interrupts at points designed into the script, instead of waiting\n920\tbehind an open chat window. The largest deployed AI tutor in the world reports that most\n921\tof its students simply never used the chat, and is now being rebuilt to be visible during\n922\tthe task.\n923\t\n924\tThe second: evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery. If a student\n925\ttakes a hint, that attempt no longer counts, and we ask a fresh question instead.\n926\t\n927\tI should be precise about where that rule comes from, because it would be easy to\n928\toverclaim. The rule itself comes from the tutoring systems of the nineteen-nineties,\n929\twhere asking for a hint already invalidated the attempt. Bastani does not test that rule.\n930\tWhat Bastani supplies is the modern warning that sent us looking for it.\n931\t\n932\tThe third, and the one I am least comfortable with: an unassisted check at the end of\n933\teach concept. No grade, never presented to the student as an assessment, feeding only the\n934\tinstructor's diagnosis.\n935\t\n936\tWe added it for one reason. Without it, a tutor that produced exactly the result of that\n937\tfirst experiment would look, in our own data, identical to an excellent one.\n938\t\n939\tAnd to be honest about where we are: none of that is a result yet. It is a set of design\n940\tdecisions that follow from other people's evidence.\n941\t\n942\tWe have not run a trial. We do not know whether our tutor teaches anything.\n943\t\n944\tWhat I can tell you is that we designed the instrument that would let us find out before\n945\twe finished the thing it measures — and I would ask the same of anyone selling to us.\n946\t\n947\t## SLIDE 15 — The line to remember\n948\t\n949\t<!--visual\n950\t<div class=\"closing\">\n951\t  <h2>The decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.</h2>\n952\t  <p class=\"closing-body\">It is a system that can say what it believes the student knows,\n953\t  why it chose the next step, when it should stay quiet — and, without being the judge of\n954\t  its own work, whether the student actually learned.</p>\n955\t</div>\n956\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our conclusion</span> <span class=\"chain\">No paper identifies “the decisive advance”. This one is mine</span></div>\n957\t-->\n958\t\n959\tIf you take one sentence away from this, I would like it to be this one.\n960\t\n961\tThe decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.\n962\t\n963\tIt is a system that can tell you what it believes the student knows, why it chose the\n964\tnext step, when it ought to stay quiet, and — without being the judge of its own work —\n965\twhether the student actually learned.\n966\t\n967\tThank you.\n968\t\n969\t## SLIDE A1 — References\n970\t\n971\t<!--visual\n972\t<h2>References</h2>\n973\t<div class=\"refs\">\n974\t<p><b>Bastani, H., Bastani, O., Sungu, A., Ge, H., Kabakcı, Ö., &amp; Mariman, R.</b> (2025).\n975\tGenerative AI without guardrails can harm learning: Evidence from high school mathematics.\n976\t<i>PNAS</i>, 122(26), e2422633122. doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2422633122</p>\n977\t<p><b>Burneo, A., Dinarte-Diaz, L., Lopez, C., &amp; Molina, E.</b> (2026). Can EdTech Close\n978\tLearning Gaps? Global Evidence from Digital Interventions. Background paper, <i>World\n979\tDevelopment Report 2026</i>, World Bank. Working paper, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n980\t<p><b>Kestin, G., Miller, K., Klales, A., Milbourne, T., &amp; Ponti, G.</b> (2025). AI tutoring\n981\toutperforms in-class active learning: An RCT introducing a novel research-based design in an\n982\tauthentic educational setting. <i>Scientific Reports</i>, 15, 17458.\n983\tdoi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6</p>\n984\t<p><b>De Simone, M., Tiberti, F., Barron Rodriguez, M., Manolio, F., Mosuro, W., &amp;\n985\tDikoru, E. J.</b> (2025). From Chalkboards to Chatbots: Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI\n986\ton Learning Outcomes in Nigeria. World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125.</p>\n987\t<p><b>LearnLM Team, Google &amp; Fab AI</b> (2026). Teaching with Gemini: Measuring the impact\n988\tof Guided Learning on student mathematics progress in Sierra Leone. Technical report\n989\t(provider-produced, not peer-reviewed).</p>\n990\t<p><b>Robinson, C. D., Gormley, D., Trindade Ribeiro, A., &amp; Loeb, S.</b> (2026). Access is\n991\tNot Enough: Human Support Improves Engagement with AI Tutoring. EdWorkingPaper 26-1451,\n992\tAnnenberg Institute, Brown University. doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388</p>\n993\t<p><b>Muralidharan, K., Singh, A., &amp; Ganimian, A. J.</b> (2019). Disrupting Education?\n994\tExperimental Evidence on Technology-Aided Instruction in India. <i>American Economic Review</i>,\n995\t109(4), 1426–1460. doi.org/10.1257/aer.20171112</p>\n996\t<p><b>Henkel, O., Horne-Robinson, H., Kozhakhmetova, N., &amp; Lee, A.</b> (2024). Effective and\n997\tScalable Math Support: Evidence on the Impact of an AI-Tutor on Math Achievement in Ghana.\n998\tarXiv:2402.09809. Preprint.</p>\n999\t<p><b>Roschelle, J., Feng, M., Murphy, R. F., &amp; Mason, C. A.</b> (2016). Online Mathematics\n1000\tHomework Increases Student Achievement. <i>AERA Open</i>, 2(4), 1–12.\n1001\tdoi.org/10.1177/2332858416673968</p>\n1002\t<p><b>Wang, R. E., Ribeiro, A. T., Robinson, C. D., Loeb, S., &amp; Demszky, D.</b> (2024).\n1003\tTutor CoPilot: A Human-AI Approach for Scaling Real-Time Expertise. arXiv:2410.03017.</p>\n1004\t<p><b>LearnLM Team, Google, &amp; Eedi</b> (2025). AI tutoring can safely and effectively\n1005\tsupport students: An exploratory RCT in UK classrooms. arXiv:2512.23633.</p>\n1006\t</div>\n1007\t-->\n1008\t\n1009\t## SLIDE A2 — How this talk was made\n1010\t\n1011\t<!--visual\n1012\t<h2>How this talk was made</h2>\n1013\t<div class=\"method\">\n1014\t<p>The source report was developed by <b>Kristian with ChatGPT</b>. Because it came from a\n1015\tlanguage model, no figure reached a slide until it had been checked against the primary\n1016\tsource.</p>\n1017\t<p class=\"role\">Numerical claims checked against primary sources by <b>fable</b> (task T-006).\n1018\tNine headline figures: eight held, one was corrected — Robinson's usage figure is\n1019\t“1 to 4 minutes”, not 4.4. Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD, not 0.24. Sierra Leone is\n1020\tquoted at its ITT of 0.258, not its ToT.</p>\n1021\t<p class=\"role\">Research questions, study contrasts and the three open questions compiled by\n1022\t<b>fable</b> (T-006), who also caught that Robinson randomised <i>added human support</i>, not\n1023\taccess — which corrected two slides.</p>\n1024\t<p class=\"role\">Prior-art research by <b>codex</b>, <b>claude</b> and <b>agy</b> (task T-002),\n1025\tacross three disjoint lanes: tutoring-system internals, interfaces and authoring, and deployed\n1026\tLLM tutors.</p>\n1027\t<p class=\"role\">Provenance taxonomy and the audit of every claim on these slides by\n1028\t<b>codex</b> (T-006), which raised seven blockers against earlier drafts — all applied.</p>\n1029\t<p class=\"role\">Talk selection, synthesis and delivery by <b>claude</b> and Kristian.\n1030\t<b>Kristian is the principal and the presenter.</b></p>\n1031\t<p>Naming the tools is not a flourish. If a number on a slide is wrong, this is the trail you\n1032\twould follow to find out why.</p>\n1033\t</div>\n1034\t-->\n1035\t\n1036\t## SLIDE A3 — Backup · Mindspark\n1037\t\n1038\t<!--visual\n1039\t<div class=\"card\">\n1040\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the pre-LLM benchmark</p>\n1041\t  <h2>“The impact of a personalized technology-aided after-school instruction program in middle-school grades in urban India, using a lottery that provided winners with free access to the program.”</h2>\n1042\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> lottery winners (free access to the after-school program) vs lottery losers. Again a package: adaptive software plus scheduled after-school sessions.</p>\n1043\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> +0.37 SD in maths and +0.23 SD in Hindi in 4.5 months, from <b>pre-language-model</b> adaptive technology. Similar absolute gains for all, larger relative gains for weaker students. This is the benchmark the generative generation has not demonstrably beaten.</p>\n1044\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> anything about generative AI — there is no language model here. That is precisely its role in this talk.</p>\n1045\t  <p class=\"cite\">Muralidharan, K., Singh, A., &amp; Ganimian, A. J. (2019). <i>American Economic Review</i>, 109(4), 1426–1460.</p>\n1046\t</div>\n1047\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the published article</span></div>\n1048\t-->\n1049\t\n1050\t## SLIDE A4 — Backup · Rori (Ghana)\n1051\t\n1052\t<!--visual\n1053\t<div class=\"card\">\n1054\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · low-bandwidth access</p>\n1055\t  <h2>“The impact of Rori, an AI powered conversational math tutor accessible via WhatsApp, on the math performance of approximately 1,000 students in grades 3–9 across 11 schools in Ghana.”</h2>\n1056\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> control continued regular maths instruction; treatment used Rori for two 30-minute sessions a week over 8 months <b>in addition to</b> regular instruction — extra time again.</p>\n1057\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> d = 0.36–0.37, on basic phones over low-bandwidth networks. The access story matters for low-resource settings.</p>\n1058\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> treating it as confirmatory. Preprint; assignment at school level with only 11 units; added instructional time uncontrolled. The authors' own caveat: “the results should be interpreted judiciously, as they only report on year 1.”</p>\n1059\t  <p class=\"cite\">Henkel, O., Horne-Robinson, H., Kozhakhmetova, N., &amp; Lee, A. (2024). arXiv:2402.09809. Preprint.</p>\n1060\t</div>\n1061\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) · preprint, treat as preliminary</span></div>\n1062\t-->\n1063\t\n1064\t## SLIDE A5 — Backup · ASSISTments\n1065\t\n1066\t<!--visual\n1067\t<div class=\"card\">\n1068\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the other pre-LLM benchmark</p>\n1069\t  <h2>“Do students in schools that use ASSISTments for mathematics homework learn more than students in schools who do homework without ASSISTments?”</h2>\n1070\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> schools randomised to ASSISTments — online homework with immediate feedback <b>plus teacher training</b> — against business-as-usual homework practices.</p>\n1071\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> g = 0.18 at state scale over a full school year; 2,850 seventh-graders, 43 schools, Maine. Students with low prior achievement benefited most.</p>\n1072\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> claims about chatbots — there is no language model. And the effect includes the teacher professional development, not just the software.</p>\n1073\t  <p class=\"cite\">Roschelle, J., Feng, M., Murphy, R. F., &amp; Mason, C. A. (2016). <i>AERA Open</i>, 2(4), 1–12.</p>\n1074\t</div>\n1075\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the published article</span></div>\n1076\t-->\n1077\t\n1078\t## SLIDE A6 — Backup · Tutor CoPilot\n1079\t\n1080\t<!--visual\n1081\t<div class=\"card\">\n1082\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · AI aimed at the tutor, not the student</p>\n1083\t  <h2>“Training novice educators with expert guidance is important for effectiveness but expensive … We introduce Tutor CoPilot, a novel Human-AI approach that leverages a model of expert thinking to provide expert-like guidance to tutors as they tutor.”</h2>\n1084\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> tutors randomised to access or no access. <b>The model never talks to the student.</b> Preregistered; 900 tutors, ~1,800 students in Title I schools, grades 3–8.</p>\n1085\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> +4 percentage points on the platform's exit ticket (62% → 66%), and most of the benefit goes to the lowest-rated tutors. A closed menu of seven human-chosen strategies is a workable division of labour.</p>\n1086\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> learning gains in general: the distal standardised test was null. The significant effect is on the platform's own progression gate.</p>\n1087\t  <p class=\"cite\">Wang, R. E., Ribeiro, A. T., Robinson, C. D., Loeb, S., &amp; Demszky, D. (2024). arXiv:2410.03017.</p>\n1088\t</div>\n1089\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Researched in T-002 (claude) · question quoted by fable in T-006</span></div>\n1090\t-->\n1091\t\n1092\t## SLIDE A7 — Backup · Eedi / LearnLM\n1093\t\n1094\t<!--visual\n1095\t<div class=\"card\">\n1096\t  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · supervised, not autonomous</p>\n1097\t  <h2>“To evaluate whether generative AI might help expand access to [one-to-one tutoring], we conducted an exploratory randomized controlled trial with N = 165 students across five UK secondary schools.”</h2>\n1098\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> two levels. Students randomised to static hints vs live 1:1 chat; within chat, sessions randomised to a human tutor alone vs <b>LearnLM drafting every message under human supervision</b> — the tutor revised each draft until they would send it themselves.</p>\n1099\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> supervised AI matched human tutors on remediation (93.0% vs 91.2%, overlapping intervals), tutors approved 76.4% of drafts with zero or minimal edits, and the abstract reports +5.5 points on novel problems.</p>\n1100\t  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> autonomous AI tutoring — a human vetted every single message. Nor anything confirmatory: the authors call it exploratory, N=165, and the author list includes the vendor and the platform.</p>\n1101\t  <p class=\"cite\">LearnLM Team, Google, &amp; Eedi (2025). arXiv:2512.23633. Exploratory.</p>\n1102\t</div>\n1103\t<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Researched in T-002 (claude) · question quoted by fable in T-006</span></div>\n1104\t-->\n1105\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c98b3399-61ca-4ebc-b12e-3776ed7417f3","uuid":"494f5c2a-5ea0-4634-a854-031747a76a49","timestamp":"2026-08-11T00:47:44.257Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/klopezva/GithubRepos/tutorIA/docs/talk/talk.en.md","content":"# A chatbot is not a tutor\n\nTalk source. 15 minutes. Audience: teaching colleagues and people who decide what\nsoftware enters a course.\n\nFormat: one `## SLIDE` block per slide. The `<!--visual ... -->` comment holds the slide\nHTML; everything else in the block is the narration, spoken in order. `build_deck.py`\nturns this file into `deck.html` plus the narration file fed to `audioexplain`.\n\n**Provenance of every number on these slides.** The source is\n`docs/sources/IA_generativa_aprendizaje_informe_completo.pdf`, which Kristian produced in\na ChatGPT conversation. Because it came from a language model, none of it went on a slide\nuntil it was checked against primary sources:\n\n- Bastani et al., Tutor CoPilot, Eedi/LearnLM and the Khan A/B results were verified in\n  task T-002 — see `coord/work/claude/T-002-carril-C-tutores-llm-juez.md`.\n- The meta-analysis, the GenAI-vs-adaptive differential, Kestin, Nigeria, Sierra Leone,\n  Mindspark, Robinson, Rori and ASSISTments were verified by `fable` in task T-006 — see\n  `coord/work/fable/T-006-verificacion-cifras.md`, which carries the page-level URL for\n  each one.\n\nThree of fable's findings are already applied here: Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD\n(not 0.24, which is why the talk quotes only the 0.31 composite); Robinson's usage figure\nis \"1 to 4 minutes\", not 4.4; and Sierra Leone's 0.380 ToT is not quoted at all, only the\n0.258 ITT. Do not add a number to a slide without adding it to one of those two files\nfirst.\n\n---\n\n## SLIDE 1 — Title\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"title-slide\">\n  <h1>A chatbot is not a tutor</h1>\n  <p class=\"sub\">What the evidence says about AI and learning — and what to ask for\n  before one enters your course</p>\n  <p class=\"meta\">Evidence cutoff: August 2026</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\nI want to start with an experiment that should make all of us a little uncomfortable.\n\nThen I will give you the honest number for whether any of this works.\n\nAnd I will finish with the questions I would ask before letting one of these tools near\nmy students.\n\n## SLIDE 1b — Where this comes from\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>Where this talk comes from</h2>\n<div class=\"pipeline\">\n  <div class=\"step\"><b>1 · Draft</b><span>A research report I generated in a\n    conversation with a language model</span></div>\n  <div class=\"step\"><b>2 · Check</b><span>9 headline figures re-checked against the\n    original papers &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>8 held, 1 was wrong</b></span></div>\n  <div class=\"step\"><b>3 · Map</b><span>An earlier review of this literature from three\n    angles: tutoring systems, interfaces and authoring, deployed LLM tutors</span></div>\n  <div class=\"step\"><b>4 · This talk</b><span>Every slide labels which of the three it\n    is showing you</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">Every claim on these slides carries one of four labels:</p>\n<div class=\"eyebrow-row\">\n  <span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· author, year</span></span>\n  <span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on A + B</span></span>\n  <span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our recommendation</span>\n  <span class=\"prov prov-p\">Our project <span class=\"src\">· not yet evaluated</span></span>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><b>Taxonomy:</b> <span class=\"chain\">designed by codex ·\nprovenance audit of all slides in coord/work/codex/T-006-procedencia.md</span></div>\n-->\n\nBefore any of the evidence, thirty seconds on where this talk comes from, because you\nshould be able to tell my sources apart from my opinions.\n\nIt started as a research report I generated in a conversation with a language model.\n\nThat is a perfectly good way to start and a terrible way to finish, so nothing from it\nwent onto a slide until it had been checked against the original paper.\n\nNine headline figures were re-checked that way. Eight held.\n\nOne was wrong, and I will show you that one later, because correcting it turned out to\nmake the argument better rather than weaker.\n\nUnderneath that sits an earlier review of this same literature from three separate\nangles: the intelligent tutoring tradition, the interface and authoring side, and the\nlanguage-model tutors that are actually deployed today.\n\nSo from here on, every claim on these slides carries one of four labels.\n\nEvidence, with the author and the year, means somebody measured it.\n\nOur synthesis means we built the conclusion by comparing sources. The citation proves the\ninputs, not the conclusion.\n\nOur recommendation means it is a judgement about what you should do, and no experiment\nestablished it.\n\nAnd our project means it is a fact about the thing we are building, which is not the same\nas a result.\n\nThe middle two are the ones you should argue with, and I have tried to make them easy to\nfind rather than easy to miss.\n\n## SLIDE 1c — The question Bastani et al. asked\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the harm experiment</p>\n  <h2>“How generative AI affects <em>learning</em> — how humans acquire new skills as\n  they perform tasks.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> three arms — an unrestricted assistant, a\n  safeguarded tutor, and practice with no technology. The lecture and the final unassisted\n  exam are identical across arms; only the practice tool changes.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> a claim about AI harming learning in general.\n  The safeguarded arm is the proof that the harm is not inevitable.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Bastani, H., Bastani, O., Sungu, A., Ge, H., Kabakcı, Ö., &amp; Mariman, R.\n  (2025). <i>PNAS</i>, 122(26), e2422633122.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the abstract</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Compiled in T-006 (fable) from the primary source</span></div>\n-->\n\nBefore the result, the question they set out to answer, in their words: how generative AI\naffects learning — how humans acquire new skills as they perform tasks.\n\nNotice that the question is about acquisition, not about performance. That is what makes\nthe design work.\n\n## SLIDE 2 — The experiment\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>One experiment, three groups</h2>\n<div class=\"arms\">\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">1</span><b>Unrestricted chat</b>\n    <span>practice with a standard assistant</span></div>\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">2</span><b>Tutored version</b>\n    <span>same model, safeguards: hints, never the answer</span></div>\n  <div class=\"arm\"><span class=\"arm-n\">3</span><b>Control</b>\n    <span>practice with no technology</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">≈1,000 high-school mathematics students, randomly assigned.\nPractice <b>with</b> the tool. Then the tool is removed and everyone sits the same exam.</p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Bastani et al. 2025, PNAS</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → researched in T-002 (claude) → cited here</span></div>\n-->\n\nNearly a thousand high school mathematics students were randomly assigned to three\ngroups.\n\nThe first group practised with an ordinary chat assistant, with no restrictions.\n\nThe second group practised with the same underlying model, but wrapped in safeguards.\nIt was built to give hints and withhold the answer.\n\nThe third group practised the way students have always practised, with no technology at\nall.\n\nThen the researchers took the assistance away, and gave all three groups the same exam.\n\nHold on to that last step, because it is the entire design. The measurement happens\nafter the help is gone.\n\n## SLIDE 3 — The result\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>What happened</h2>\n<table class=\"results\">\n  <tr><th></th><th>During practice<br><span>with the tool</span></th>\n      <th>On the exam<br><span>without it</span></th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Unrestricted chat</b></td>\n      <td class=\"up\">+48%</td><td class=\"down\">−17%</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Tutored, with safeguards</b></td>\n      <td class=\"up\">+127%</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ control</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"note\">Both relative to the control group. Safeguards removed the harm.\nThey did not produce an advantage.</p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Bastani et al. 2025, PNAS</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → verified in T-002 (claude)</span></div>\n-->\n\nDuring the practice sessions, the unrestricted group performed forty-eight per cent\nbetter than the control group.\n\nOn the exam, without the tool, the same students performed seventeen per cent worse than\nthe control group.\n\nNow look at the tutored version, the one built carefully, with safeguards.\n\nDuring practice it did even better. A hundred and twenty-seven per cent above control.\n\nAnd on the exam it came out level with the control group. No better, no worse.\n\nSo the careful design succeeded at something real. It removed the damage. What it did\nnot do was produce any measurable learning advantage of its own.\n\n## SLIDE 4 — The lesson\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The lesson</p>\n  <h2>Performance <em>with</em> the tool is not learning.</h2>\n  <p class=\"sub\">The number that looked most like success was the number that predicted\n  it least.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Bastani + the ITS literature</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">No paper states this in these words; the inference is mine</span></div>\n-->\n\nI want to be careful about what this does and does not show.\n\nIt is not evidence that artificial intelligence damages learning in general. The second\ngroup is the proof that it does not have to.\n\nIt is evidence about measurement, and that makes it more useful, not less.\n\nThe moment that looked most like success — a student solving more problems, faster, with\nhelp available — was the moment that told us least about whether anything had been\nlearned.\n\nAnd here is the practical consequence for anyone choosing software. If you evaluate a\ntool by watching students use it, that can lead you to prefer the tools that do the most\nwork for them.\n\nI am not claiming that is what buyers systematically do — nobody has measured that. I am\nclaiming this experiment shows the metric is capable of inverting the ranking.\n\nWhich is enough reason to be a little suspicious of a demonstration that feels\nwonderful.\n\n## SLIDE 4b — The question the meta-analysis asked\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the meta-analysis</p>\n  <h2>“Adaptive educational technology promises the personalization of teaching at a\n  fraction of its cost, but experimental estimates vary widely in magnitude and in sign,\n  and existing syntheses <em>predate generative AI</em>.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>What they did:</b> brought computer-assisted learning platforms and\n  generative AI tools into a common framework, under common inclusion criteria, on a common\n  effect-size scale.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> treating the subgroups as settled. The authors\n  call them suggestive rather than conclusive, and no included study is from a low-income\n  country.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Burneo, A., Dinarte-Diaz, L., Lopez, C., &amp; Molina, E. (2026). Background\n  paper, <i>World Development Report 2026</i>, World Bank. Working paper.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the abstract</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the primary PDF</span></div>\n-->\n\nAnd the same for the meta-analysis, because its question tells you what it can settle.\n\nThey note that experimental estimates vary widely in magnitude and even in sign, and that\nthe existing syntheses all predate generative AI.\n\nSo what they built is a common frame: old-style adaptive platforms and new generative\ntools, under the same inclusion criteria, on the same effect-size scale.\n\nThat is what makes the next two slides possible, and it is also the reason they can\ncompare the generations at all.\n\n## SLIDE 5 — So does it work?\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>So does it work?</h2>\n<div class=\"stat-row\">\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.125</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">average effect on learning<br>vs traditional instruction</span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">14</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">randomised trials<br>191 effect sizes, 10 economies</span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+0.12</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">the AI tutoring<br>and instruction subset</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">A real effect. Above the median for field interventions in education.\nNot a revolution. <b>Read the base honestly:</b> a World Bank working paper, not yet\npeer-reviewed; 14 studies; no included study from a low-income country.</p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo et al. 2026, World Bank</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Working paper, not peer-reviewed → verified in T-006 (fable) · the “not a revolution” judgement is mine</span></div>\n-->\n\nSo, does any of it work? Yes, on average, modestly, and the honest number is not\nthrilling.\n\nThe most recent meta-analysis pools a hundred and ninety-one effect estimates from\nfourteen randomised trials across ten economies.\n\nThe average effect on learning is about an eighth of a standard deviation. Plus zero\npoint one two five.\n\nThe subset that is specifically artificial intelligence tutoring or instruction sits at\nroughly the same value.\n\nThat is a genuine effect, and it sits above the median for education interventions\nmeasured in real classrooms. It is worth having.\n\nIt is also not a transformation of education, and anyone selling you one is running\nahead of the evidence.\n\nAnd since I am going to spend the rest of this talk being demanding, let me be fair for\na moment about what this technology genuinely does well.\n\nIt is available at eleven at night, which no tutor of mine ever was.\n\nIt is endlessly patient, and it does not sigh when a student asks the same question for\nthe third time.\n\nIt will explain the same idea in four different ways, in the student's own language, at\nwhatever reading level they need.\n\nThose are real gains in access, and for some students access is the whole problem.\n\nMy argument is not that this is worthless. It is that access and availability are not the\nsame thing as instruction, and the evidence only rewards us when we build the second one\non top of the first.\n\n## SLIDE 6 — The number nobody quotes\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The number that never appears in a pitch</p>\n  <h2>Generative AI vs the previous generation of adaptive tutors</h2>\n  <div class=\"stat-inline\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0.022</span><span class=\"stat-u\">SD</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">estimated difference &nbsp;·&nbsp; standard error <b>0.075</b>\n    &nbsp;·&nbsp; CI [−0.15, 0.19]</span></div>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Not evidence of equivalence. Absence of a demonstrated advantage.</p>\n  <p class=\"note\">The authors' own words: the interval <em>“bounds the difference between\n  generations rather than resolving it”</em> — and “the experimental record to date shows\n  no advantage for the newer technology.”</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo et al. 2026 — authors’ own wording</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Primary source → verified in T-006 (fable) · “still the benchmark” is my reading</span></div>\n-->\n\nHere is the number that almost never appears in a product presentation.\n\nWhen you compare generative systems against the previous generation of adaptive tutoring\nsoftware — the kind that existed well before large language models — the estimated\ndifference is about two hundredths of a standard deviation.\n\nThe standard error on that estimate is more than three times the estimate itself.\n\nI want to state that carefully, because it is easy to overclaim in either direction.\n\nThis is not proof that the two are equivalent. The evidence base is simply not yet able\nto rank the generations.\n\nBut it does mean something concrete for a purchasing decision. The older, less\nconversational, less impressive-looking technology is still the benchmark, and it has not\nbeen beaten.\n\n## SLIDE 7 — The average hides the lesson\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The average is the least interesting part</h2>\n<table class=\"results wide\">\n  <tr><th>Study</th><th>Effect</th><th>What was actually deployed</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>University physics, crossover</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.63 SD</td>\n      <td>expert-written lessons, verified solutions, sequence enforced by the platform</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Nigeria, after-school</td><td class=\"up\">≈ +0.31 SD</td>\n      <td>AI + extra time + teachers present + students working in pairs</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Sierra Leone, school-scale</td><td class=\"up\">+0.258 SD</td>\n      <td>teacher-led classes; teachers set the objectives</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>AI reading tutor + a human<br>to drive engagement</td><td class=\"flat\">≈ 0</td>\n      <td><b>both arms had the platform</b>; what was randomised was adding a person whose\n      job was engagement, not teaching</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p class=\"note\">Read each honestly: the physics result is two lessons with an immediate\ntest at one elite university; Nigeria lost 569 of 1,328 students before the final test;\nSierra Leone is a provider report, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Kestin 2025 · De Simone 2025 · LearnLM/Fab AI 2026 · Robinson 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">All four verified in T-006 (fable) · Sierra Leone is a provider report</span></div>\n-->\n\nThe average is the least interesting part of that meta-analysis, because the spread\naround it is enormous.\n\nA crossover study in university physics found about six tenths of a standard deviation.\n\nAn after-school programme in Nigeria found about three tenths.\n\nA school-scale deployment in Sierra Leone found a quarter of a standard deviation.\n\nAnd a pair of trials on an AI reading tutor found essentially nothing on reading.\n\nThat last row needs a caveat, and I would rather give it to you than have you find it\nafterwards. In those trials both groups already had the platform. What was randomised was\nadding a human being whose job was to keep students engaged with it. So the null is about\nthe human support, not about access itself.\n\nSame broad technology. Completely different outcomes. So the interesting question is not\nwhether AI works. It is what distinguishes the top row from the bottom one.\n\n## SLIDE 7b — What each of those three actually asked\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>Three studies, three different questions</h2>\n<div class=\"qgrid\">\n  <div><b>Kestin et al. — physics</b>\n    <span>“Measure the difference between how much students learn … when <em>identical\n    material</em> is presented through an AI tutor compared with an active learning\n    classroom.”<br><br>Not AI versus nothing. Not AI versus a lecture. AI versus the best\n    thing we already do.</span></div>\n  <div><b>De Simone et al. — Nigeria</b>\n    <span>“Whether generative AI … can help solve that problem” — large learning deficits\n    with scarce teaching resources.<br><br>The control “did not receive any intervention”.\n    So +0.31 SD is the effect of the whole after-school package, not of the model.</span></div>\n  <div><b>LearnLM/Fab AI — Sierra Leone</b>\n    <span>“How does the <em>teacher-led integration</em> of Guided Learning into mathematics\n    classes … affect student learning outcomes?”<br><br>Same teacher training in both arms.\n    The question is about integration, not about the app.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· questions quoted from each paper</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Compiled and checked in T-006 (fable) · Sierra Leone is a provider report</span></div>\n-->\n\nIt is worth putting the three questions side by side, because they are not the same\nquestion and the effect sizes are not comparable in the way a table makes them look.\n\nKestin asked whether an AI tutor beats an active learning classroom when the material is\nidentical. Not AI against nothing, and not AI against a lecture — AI against the best\nthing we already know how to do. That is the hardest comparison in the set, and it is why\nthat number is striking.\n\nNigeria asked whether generative AI can help with large learning deficits and scarce\nteaching resources. Their control group received no intervention at all. So the three\ntenths is the effect of the entire after-school package, not of the model inside it.\n\nSierra Leone asked something different again: how does teacher-led integration of the tool\ninto maths classes affect outcomes. Both arms had the same teacher training. The question\nthere is about integration, not about the app.\n\nThree questions, three contrasts, one table. That is exactly how a literature gets\nmisread.\n\n## SLIDE 8 — Effects are recipes\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <h2>Across these cases, the large effects look like <em>recipes</em>,\n  not properties of the model.</h2>\n  <ul class=\"clean\">\n    <li>Content written and checked by subject experts</li>\n    <li>A sequence the platform enforces from <b>outside</b> the model</li>\n    <li>Extra time that was actually scheduled</li>\n    <li>A teacher or facilitator in the room</li>\n  </ul>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Copying the brand does not reproduce the treatment.\n  Neither does copying the prompt.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Kestin + Nigeria + Sierra Leone</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">The ingredients are measured; that they form a necessary recipe is not</span></div>\n-->\n\nLook at what was actually deployed in these four cases, and a pattern appears.\n\nThe physics result used lessons designed by subject experts, with worked solutions\nprepared in advance, and a sequence the platform imposed from outside the model.\n\nThat detail deserves a moment. The researchers reported that the prompt alone could not\nhold the teaching sequence reliably, so they moved the sequence out of the prompt and\ninto the software around it.\n\nThe Nigerian programme was after-school sessions, with additional instructional time,\nteachers present, and students working in pairs.\n\nSierra Leone was ordinary classes, led by teachers who set the objectives.\n\nAcross these high-effect cases, what was tested is a package: verified content, an\nenforced sequence, protected time, and people.\n\nI want to be careful here, because this is my reading and not a finding. Nobody has\ntested whether those four ingredients are each necessary, and Sierra Leone does not\ncontain all of them.\n\nWhat I am confident saying is the negative version. Copying the brand does not reproduce\nthe treatment. And copying the prompt does not reproduce it either.\n\n## SLIDE 8b — The question Robinson et al. asked\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the take-up null</p>\n  <h2>“An engaged, in-person human tutor whose role is to support student engagement with\n  an AI literacy tutor, <em>not to deliver direct instruction</em>.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> students used the AI literacy platform\n  independently, or with that in-person tutor. <b>Both arms had the platform.</b></p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> “access to an AI tutor produced zero learning”\n  as an experimental result. There is no no-AI arm, so this design cannot estimate access\n  against nothing.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Robinson, C. D., Gormley, D., Trindade Ribeiro, A., &amp; Loeb, S. (2026).\n  EdWorkingPaper 26-1451, Annenberg Institute, Brown University.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· research question quoted from the introduction</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">This card exists because fable caught the talk misreading the contrast (T-006)</span></div>\n-->\n\nAnd this one I want to give you carefully, because an earlier version of this talk got it\nwrong, and it is the kind of wrong that gets found in the question session rather than in\nthe preparation.\n\nWhat they tested was an in-person tutor whose role was to support engagement with an AI\nliteracy tutor — explicitly not to deliver instruction.\n\nBoth groups had the platform. What was randomised was the human being.\n\nSo this study cannot tell you what happens when you compare access against nothing,\nbecause there is no arm without the AI.\n\n## SLIDE 9 — The null result that matters most\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The metric that moved, and the one that didn't</h2>\n<p class=\"lede\">Two RCTs, ≈350 primary students. <b>Both arms had the AI tutor.</b> What\nwas randomised: adding an in-person tutor whose role was engagement, not instruction.</p>\n<div class=\"stat-row\">\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+71–80%</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">engagement<br><b>the number that goes in the deck</b></span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">+1 to 4</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">minutes of actual use<br>per week &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>the real dose</b></span></div>\n  <div class=\"stat\"><span class=\"stat-n\">0</span>\n    <span class=\"stat-l\">improvement<br>in reading</span></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"note\">Descriptive, not randomised: in the access-only arm nearly half never\nopened the platform, and those who did averaged 2–5 minutes a week.\n<b>A percentage is not minutes.</b></p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Robinson et al. 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Correction trail: source report said 4.4 min → primary abstract says 1–4 → corrected after verification in T-006 (fable)</span></div>\n-->\n\nThe most useful result in this area is a null one, and it is the least discussed.\n\nTwo randomised trials, around three hundred and fifty primary school children. Both\ngroups had the AI reading tutor. The thing being tested was adding a person whose job was\nto keep them using it.\n\nEngagement rose by between seventy-one and eighty per cent.\n\nThat is the number that goes in the deck. Now the same result in absolute terms.\n\nAverage use went up by one to four minutes per week.\n\nAnd reading did not improve at all.\n\nI find this the most clarifying pair of numbers in the whole literature, because both are\ntrue and they tell opposite stories.\n\nA relative rise in engagement of eighty per cent sounds like adoption. One to four\nminutes a week is not a dose of anything.\n\nThere is one more number here, and I want to label it carefully because it is descriptive\nrather than randomised. In the arm that had the platform and no human support, nearly half\nthe students never opened it at all, and those who did averaged two to five minutes a\nweek.\n\nSo keep this one in your pocket for the next time someone proposes buying licences for\neverybody. Take-up is not an implementation detail. It is part of the treatment, and it\nhas to be planned and measured like one.\n\n## SLIDE 10 — What is under the conversation\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>The conversation is the surface</h2>\n<div class=\"stack\">\n  <div class=\"layer surface\">Conversation and interface</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Guardrails — attempt before hint, no answer on demand, fading</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Tools and verification — calculation and facts <b>outside</b> the model</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Pedagogical policy — what to do next, and why</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Learner model — a structured estimate, not a chat history</div>\n  <div class=\"layer\">Domain model — concepts, prerequisites, worked solutions</div>\n  <div class=\"layer base\">Human orchestration and independent measurement</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· design framework, condensed from the source report §3</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">A recommended architecture, not a measured effect</span></div>\n-->\n\nIf the effects come from packages, it is worth asking what a package actually contains.\n\nThe conversation is the surface. It is the part you see in a demonstration, and usually\nthe only part a demonstration shows.\n\nUnderneath, a serious system needs a map of the subject, with prerequisites and worked\nsolutions.\n\nIt needs a structured estimate of what this particular student knows. Not a\nconversation history — an actual state, that can be inspected.\n\nThat distinction is worth making concrete, because it is where most products quietly\nfail.\n\nA chat history says: this student asked about the budget constraint twice and seemed\nconfused.\n\nA learner model, in the system we are building, could instead say: this student can\ncompute the slope, cannot interpret it as an opportunity cost, has not demonstrated the\nfeasible set at all, and the last two pieces of evidence came with hints, so they do not\ncount.\n\nThe first is a transcript. The second is something a teacher can read, disagree with, and\ncorrect.\n\nIt needs an explicit policy for what to do next, and a reason for choosing it.\n\nIt needs tools that calculate and verify, sitting outside the model that does the\ntalking.\n\nIt needs limits on what it is willing to reveal, and support that withdraws as the\nstudent improves.\n\nAnd it needs a teacher who can see what it believes and overrule it, plus a way of\nmeasuring itself that is not its own opinion.\n\n## SLIDE 11 — Four levels of personalization\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>Four levels of \"personalized\"</h2>\n<ol class=\"levels\">\n  <li><b>Presentation</b> — tone, language, length, readability\n      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n  <li><b>Interaction</b> — the example, the hint, the next question in this turn\n      <span class=\"tag common\">common</span></li>\n  <li><b>Pedagogical decision</b> — the next action, chosen from a diagnosis\n      <span class=\"tag rare\">evidence thin</span></li>\n  <li><b>Trajectory</b> — across sessions: prerequisites, forgetting, transfer\n      <span class=\"tag rare\">evidence thin</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"note big\">Remembering your name is not the same as estimating what you know.</p>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our framework <span class=\"src\">· our taxonomy, not a validated one</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Public causal evidence does not isolate levels 3–4; the split is ours</span></div>\n-->\n\nThe word personalized is doing a great deal of unearned work in this field, so it helps\nto break it into four levels.\n\nLevel one changes presentation: tone, language, length, reading level.\n\nLevel two changes the interaction: which example, which hint, which question comes next\nin this turn.\n\nMany of the products you will be shown stop there, and both levels are genuinely useful\nfor access.\n\nLevel three chooses the next action based on a diagnosis of what this student got wrong,\nand why.\n\nLevel four maintains a trajectory across sessions, tracking prerequisites, forgetting and\ntransfer.\n\nThree and four are what this framework treats as genuine pedagogical adaptation. I should\nbe careful about how I put the next part: the public causal evidence does not isolate\nthose levels and tell us the effects live there. It is thinner than that.\n\nThe test I find most useful is this. A system that remembers your name, adopts a warm\ntone, and rephrases the same content can feel deeply personal while estimating nothing at\nall about what you know.\n\n## SLIDE 12 — What to ask for\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The one question that separates a system from a demo</p>\n  <h2>\"Show me the learner model before and after three answers.<br>\n  What changed, and why?\"</h2>\n</div>\n<ol class=\"checks\">\n  <li>Curriculum traceable to sources you authorised</li>\n  <li>A student state the teacher can see — and correct when it is wrong</li>\n  <li>An explicit help policy: attempt before hint, no answer on demand, support that fades</li>\n  <li>Calculation and fact-checking done by tools, outside the language model</li>\n  <li>Evidence on your population, on your outcome, measured without the tool present</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our recommendation <span class=\"src\">· my 5-point shortlist, condensed from the report’s 10 requirements</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Normative. No experiment established this list</span></div>\n-->\n\nSo here is what I would ask for, and it fits on a single slide.\n\nStart with one question, because it separates a real system from a good demonstration.\n\nShow me the student model before and after three answers. Tell me what changed, and why\nit changed.\n\nIf the answer is a longer chat history, you are looking at level two dressed as level\nfour.\n\nThen five checks.\n\nIs the curriculum traceable to sources you authorised, rather than to the open web.\n\nCan a teacher see the estimated state, and correct it when it is wrong.\n\nIs there an explicit help policy — an attempt required before a hint, no answer on\ndemand, and support that fades as the student improves.\n\nAre calculation and fact-checking done by tools outside the language model, rather than\nby the model marking its own arithmetic.\n\nAnd is there evidence on a population like yours, on an outcome like yours, measured\nwithout the tool in the room.\n\nIf you only have two minutes with a vendor, there is a faster probe. Sit down in front of\nthe system and ask it, plainly, to just give you the answer.\n\nThen ask again, and be a little rude about it.\n\nWhat you are testing is whether the refusal is a policy or a personality. A policy holds\non the third request. A personality folds, and every one of your students will find that\nout before the end of the first week.\n\n## SLIDE 13 — How to measure\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">The measurement rule</p>\n  <h2>Independent test. No AI present. Ideally after a delay.</h2>\n  <p class=\"sub\">Everything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems per hour — measures\n  productivity. Worth tracking. Not the outcome.</p>\n  <p class=\"note big\">And count real minutes of use. A licence is not a dose.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-r\">Our measurement rule <span class=\"src\">· informed by Bastani + the evaluation framework</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">A standard we recommend, not an estimate</span></div>\n-->\n\nAnd on that last point, the measurement rule is short enough to remember on the way out.\n\nThe primary outcome should be an independent test, taken without the system present, and\nideally after a delay of days or weeks.\n\nEverything else — satisfaction, engagement, problems completed per hour, next-question\naccuracy — measures productivity while assisted.\n\nThose are worth tracking. I track them. They are simply not the outcome, and the first\nexperiment I showed you is what happens when they are treated as one.\n\nAnd count real minutes of use, by student. Not licences issued, not accounts created.\n\n## SLIDE 13b — Three questions nobody has answered\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>Three questions nobody has answered</h2>\n<p class=\"lede\">Not a wish-list. Each one is unanswered, would change what you do next\nsemester, and has a reason it has not been asked.</p>\n<ol class=\"questions\">\n  <li><b>Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?</b>\n    <span>No trial has an active control: same extra time, same structure, same adult\n    supervision, without the AI.</span></li>\n  <li><b>Generative or the previous generation — which, for the same content and time?</b>\n    <span>The head-to-head trial does not exist. Our headline 0.022 is a cross-study\n    comparison, not a race.</span></li>\n  <li><b>Is take-up itself the treatment?</b>\n    <span>Nobody has held the tool constant and randomised how it enters the classroom.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· gaps identified in T-006 (fable)</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Each gap checked against the studies in the record</span></div>\n-->\n\nI want to end the evidence part with the three questions that nobody has answered, because\nfor this audience they are more useful than the answers we do have.\n\nEach one had to clear a bar: no study in the record answers it, an answer would change\nwhat you do next semester, and I can tell you why it has not been asked.\n\n## SLIDE 13c — Question one: the missing control\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Question one</p>\n  <h2>Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?</h2>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qgrid\">\n  <div><b>The gap</b><span>Every large positive effect is a bundle — AI <em>plus</em> extra\n    scheduled time, structure and adult attention — measured against a control that got no\n    extra anything. Nigeria's control received no intervention; treatment got 18 extra hours\n    of supervised sessions. Rori added two 30-minute sessions on top of normal class.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>If most of the gain is the scheduled, supervised\n    practice block, you can run that block without buying anything. If it is the AI, the\n    licence is the cheapest ingredient. Opposite budget decisions.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>An active control roughly doubles cost and answers a\n    question neither vendors nor implementing researchers are motivated to ask — in the\n    meta-analysis, researchers implemented 16 of 19 interventions.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· De Simone 2025 · Henkel 2024 · Burneo 2026</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Designs are from the papers; that the gap matters for your budget is my inference</span></div>\n-->\n\nQuestion one. Does the model add anything beyond the package it ships in?\n\nEvery large positive effect in this literature is a bundle. The AI, plus extra scheduled\ntime, plus structure, plus adult attention — measured against a control group that got no\nextra anything.\n\nIn Nigeria the control received no intervention at all, while the treatment group got\neighteen extra hours of supervised sessions. In Ghana it was two extra half-hour sessions\na week on top of normal class.\n\nSo nobody has run the arm that would settle it: same extra time, same structure, same\nadult supervision, and no AI.\n\nAnd notice why this matters for your budget. If most of that third of a standard deviation\ncomes from the scheduled, supervised practice block, then you can run that block without\nbuying anything at all. If it comes from the software, the licence is the cheapest\ningredient in the recipe.\n\nThose are opposite decisions, and today the evidence cannot separate them.\n\nWhy has nobody done it? An active control roughly doubles the cost of the study, and it\nanswers a question that neither the vendors nor the implementing researchers are\nespecially motivated to ask. In the meta-analysis I showed you, the researchers themselves\nimplemented sixteen of the nineteen interventions.\n\n## SLIDE 13d — Question two: the race that was never run\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Question two</p>\n  <h2>Generative, or the previous generation — for the same content, in the same time?</h2>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qgrid\">\n  <div><b>The gap</b><span>No study randomises students between a pre-LLM adaptive platform\n    and a generative tutor teaching the same curriculum in the same time. The 0.022 from\n    earlier is a <em>cross-study</em> estimate: different trials, populations, subjects and\n    dosages.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>This is the actual procurement decision. The\n    auditable, cheaper, non-conversational lineage benchmarks at +0.18 to +0.37 SD. Today\n    the choice between them is made on demos.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>It needs two production-grade systems on identical\n    content, and it settles a comparison no vendor wants to lose. A meta-analysis can only\n    synthesise the experiments that exist.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· Burneo 2026 · Muralidharan 2019 · Roschelle 2016</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">The absence of a head-to-head trial was established in T-006 (fable)</span></div>\n-->\n\nQuestion two. Generative, or the previous generation, for the same content in the same\ntime?\n\nI showed you zero point zero two two earlier, and I owe you a clarification about what\nkind of number that is. It is a cross-study comparison — different trials, different\npopulations, different subjects, different dosages — not a race between two systems.\n\nThe race has never been run. No study randomises students between a pre-language-model\nadaptive platform and a generative tutor teaching the same curriculum in the same amount\nof time.\n\nAnd this is the actual purchasing decision on the table. The older, auditable, cheaper,\nnon-conversational lineage benchmarks between about zero point one eight and zero point\nthree seven of a standard deviation. That is not a weak competitor.\n\nSo today the choice between the two generations gets made on the strength of a\ndemonstration, because the head-to-head number does not exist.\n\nIt is unanswered because it needs two production-grade systems built on identical content,\nwhich is expensive, and because it settles a comparison that no vendor wants to lose.\n\n## SLIDE 13e — Question three: is take-up the treatment?\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"big-idea\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Question three</p>\n  <h2>Is take-up itself the treatment?</h2>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qgrid\">\n  <div><b>The gap</b><span>Integration mode tracks outcomes almost perfectly — mandated,\n    teacher-led, scheduled use gives +0.26 to +0.31 SD; optional access gives near-zero use.\n    But that comparison runs across different tools, subjects and countries, so integration\n    is confounded with everything.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it matters to you</b><span>It answers whether putting a link in the syllabus is\n    an intervention or a placebo — and whether the scarce resource to plan for is the tool\n    or the protected time in which students must use it.</span></div>\n  <div><b>Why it is unanswered</b><span>The field logs usage as an implementation covariate\n    instead of designing it as the treatment. And randomising timetable policy inside one\n    school raises fairness objections that after-school add-ons quietly avoid.</span></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our synthesis <span class=\"src\">· based on Sierra Leone + Nigeria + Robinson</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Integration mode is confounded across studies — that is the point, not a finding</span></div>\n-->\n\nQuestion three, and it is the one I would most like someone in this room to answer. Is\ntake-up itself the treatment?\n\nAcross these studies, how the tool enters the classroom tracks the outcome almost\nperfectly. Mandated, teacher-led, scheduled use gives you a quarter to a third of a\nstandard deviation. Optional access gives you almost no use at all.\n\nBut that comparison runs across different tools, different subjects and different\ncountries, so the integration mode is confounded with everything else.\n\nNobody has held the tool constant and randomised the policy: scheduled use inside class,\nagainst recommended optional access.\n\nFor a department, that is the question with money attached. It tells you whether putting a\nlink in the syllabus is a real intervention or a placebo, and whether the scarce resource\nyou should be protecting is not the software at all, but the timetabled hour in which\nstudents are actually required to use it.\n\nIt is unanswered partly through a blind spot — the field records usage as an\nimplementation detail rather than designing it as the treatment — and partly because\nrandomising timetable policy inside one school raises fairness objections that an\nafter-school add-on quietly avoids.\n\nAnd there is a fourth question I will mention only in passing, because it is the one that\nshould embarrass all of us. The proposition that motivates this entire literature is that\npersonalisation can be delivered at a fraction of the cost of human tutoring. Only two of\nthe fourteen studies report per-student costs on a comparable basis. Human tutoring pools\nat around zero point two nine of a standard deviation, against zero point one two for the\nAI subgroup. Whether AI wins per dollar — which is the only frame in which a department\nactually decides — has essentially never been measured next to the effects it is meant to\njustify.\n\n## SLIDE 14 — Applying it to our own work\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>What this changed in our own project</h2>\n<p class=\"lede\"><b>tutorIA</b> — a small interactive tutor for intermediate\nmicroeconomics: narrated explanation, a manipulable budget line, ungraded questions,\nand a judge that diagnoses misconceptions by name.</p>\n<ol class=\"changes\">\n  <li><b>The tutor interrupts at designed points</b>, instead of waiting at an open chat\n  window — the largest deployed AI tutor reports most students simply never used the\n  chat.</li>\n  <li><b>Evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery</b> — a hint\n  invalidates the attempt, and we re-test with a fresh item.</li>\n  <li><b>An unassisted check at the end of each concept</b> — no grade, never shown to\n  the student as an assessment, feeding only the instructor's diagnosis.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-p\">Our project <span class=\"src\">· evidence-informed decisions, not results</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">tutorIA has not been evaluated. No trial has been run</span></div>\n-->\n\nLet me close by applying this to my own work, because it is easy to give this talk and\nthen not follow it.\n\nWe are building a small interactive tutor for intermediate microeconomics. Narrated\nexplanation, a budget line the student can drag, ungraded questions, and a component that\ndiagnoses misconceptions by name.\n\nReading this evidence changed three decisions.\n\nThe first: the tutor interrupts at points designed into the script, instead of waiting\nbehind an open chat window. The largest deployed AI tutor in the world reports that most\nof its students simply never used the chat, and is now being rebuilt to be visible during\nthe task.\n\nThe second: evidence obtained with help does not count towards mastery. If a student\ntakes a hint, that attempt no longer counts, and we ask a fresh question instead.\n\nI should be precise about where that rule comes from, because it would be easy to\noverclaim. The rule itself comes from the tutoring systems of the nineteen-nineties,\nwhere asking for a hint already invalidated the attempt. Bastani does not test that rule.\nWhat Bastani supplies is the modern warning that sent us looking for it.\n\nThe third, and the one I am least comfortable with: an unassisted check at the end of\neach concept. No grade, never presented to the student as an assessment, feeding only the\ninstructor's diagnosis.\n\nWe added it for one reason. Without it, a tutor that produced exactly the result of that\nfirst experiment would look, in our own data, identical to an excellent one.\n\nAnd to be honest about where we are: none of that is a result yet. It is a set of design\ndecisions that follow from other people's evidence.\n\nWe have not run a trial. We do not know whether our tutor teaches anything.\n\nWhat I can tell you is that we designed the instrument that would let us find out before\nwe finished the thing it measures — and I would ask the same of anyone selling to us.\n\n## SLIDE 15 — The line to remember\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"closing\">\n  <h2>The decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.</h2>\n  <p class=\"closing-body\">It is a system that can say what it believes the student knows,\n  why it chose the next step, when it should stay quiet — and, without being the judge of\n  its own work, whether the student actually learned.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-s\">Our conclusion</span> <span class=\"chain\">No paper identifies “the decisive advance”. This one is mine</span></div>\n-->\n\nIf you take one sentence away from this, I would like it to be this one.\n\nThe decisive advance is not a system that sounds more like a teacher.\n\nIt is a system that can tell you what it believes the student knows, why it chose the\nnext step, when it ought to stay quiet, and — without being the judge of its own work —\nwhether the student actually learned.\n\nThank you.\n\n## SLIDE A1 — References\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>References</h2>\n<div class=\"refs\">\n<p><b>Bastani, H., Bastani, O., Sungu, A., Ge, H., Kabakcı, Ö., &amp; Mariman, R.</b> (2025).\nGenerative AI without guardrails can harm learning: Evidence from high school mathematics.\n<i>PNAS</i>, 122(26), e2422633122. doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2422633122</p>\n<p><b>Burneo, A., Dinarte-Diaz, L., Lopez, C., &amp; Molina, E.</b> (2026). Can EdTech Close\nLearning Gaps? Global Evidence from Digital Interventions. Background paper, <i>World\nDevelopment Report 2026</i>, World Bank. Working paper, not peer-reviewed.</p>\n<p><b>Kestin, G., Miller, K., Klales, A., Milbourne, T., &amp; Ponti, G.</b> (2025). AI tutoring\noutperforms in-class active learning: An RCT introducing a novel research-based design in an\nauthentic educational setting. <i>Scientific Reports</i>, 15, 17458.\ndoi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6</p>\n<p><b>De Simone, M., Tiberti, F., Barron Rodriguez, M., Manolio, F., Mosuro, W., &amp;\nDikoru, E. J.</b> (2025). From Chalkboards to Chatbots: Evaluating the Impact of Generative AI\non Learning Outcomes in Nigeria. World Bank Policy Research Working Paper 11125.</p>\n<p><b>LearnLM Team, Google &amp; Fab AI</b> (2026). Teaching with Gemini: Measuring the impact\nof Guided Learning on student mathematics progress in Sierra Leone. Technical report\n(provider-produced, not peer-reviewed).</p>\n<p><b>Robinson, C. D., Gormley, D., Trindade Ribeiro, A., &amp; Loeb, S.</b> (2026). Access is\nNot Enough: Human Support Improves Engagement with AI Tutoring. EdWorkingPaper 26-1451,\nAnnenberg Institute, Brown University. doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388</p>\n<p><b>Muralidharan, K., Singh, A., &amp; Ganimian, A. J.</b> (2019). Disrupting Education?\nExperimental Evidence on Technology-Aided Instruction in India. <i>American Economic Review</i>,\n109(4), 1426–1460. doi.org/10.1257/aer.20171112</p>\n<p><b>Henkel, O., Horne-Robinson, H., Kozhakhmetova, N., &amp; Lee, A.</b> (2024). Effective and\nScalable Math Support: Evidence on the Impact of an AI-Tutor on Math Achievement in Ghana.\narXiv:2402.09809. Preprint.</p>\n<p><b>Roschelle, J., Feng, M., Murphy, R. F., &amp; Mason, C. A.</b> (2016). Online Mathematics\nHomework Increases Student Achievement. <i>AERA Open</i>, 2(4), 1–12.\ndoi.org/10.1177/2332858416673968</p>\n<p><b>Wang, R. E., Ribeiro, A. T., Robinson, C. D., Loeb, S., &amp; Demszky, D.</b> (2024).\nTutor CoPilot: A Human-AI Approach for Scaling Real-Time Expertise. arXiv:2410.03017.</p>\n<p><b>LearnLM Team, Google, &amp; Eedi</b> (2025). AI tutoring can safely and effectively\nsupport students: An exploratory RCT in UK classrooms. arXiv:2512.23633.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A2 — How this talk was made\n\n<!--visual\n<h2>How this talk was made</h2>\n<div class=\"method\">\n<p>The source report was developed by <b>Kristian with ChatGPT</b>. Because it came from a\nlanguage model, no figure reached a slide until it had been checked against the primary\nsource.</p>\n<p class=\"role\">Numerical claims checked against primary sources by <b>fable</b> (task T-006).\nNine headline figures: eight held, one was corrected — Robinson's usage figure is\n“1 to 4 minutes”, not 4.4. Nigeria's English effect is 0.23 SD, not 0.24. Sierra Leone is\nquoted at its ITT of 0.258, not its ToT.</p>\n<p class=\"role\">Research questions, study contrasts and the three open questions compiled by\n<b>fable</b> (T-006), who also caught that Robinson randomised <i>added human support</i>, not\naccess — which corrected two slides.</p>\n<p class=\"role\">Prior-art research by <b>codex</b>, <b>claude</b> and <b>agy</b> (task T-002),\nacross three disjoint lanes: tutoring-system internals, interfaces and authoring, and deployed\nLLM tutors.</p>\n<p class=\"role\">Provenance taxonomy and the audit of every claim on these slides by\n<b>codex</b> (T-006), which raised seven blockers against earlier drafts — all applied.</p>\n<p class=\"role\">Talk selection, synthesis and delivery by <b>claude</b> and Kristian.\n<b>Kristian is the principal and the presenter.</b></p>\n<p>Naming the tools is not a flourish. If a number on a slide is wrong, this is the trail you\nwould follow to find out why.</p>\n</div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A3 — Backup · Mindspark\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the pre-LLM benchmark</p>\n  <h2>“The impact of a personalized technology-aided after-school instruction program in middle-school grades in urban India, using a lottery that provided winners with free access to the program.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> lottery winners (free access to the after-school program) vs lottery losers. Again a package: adaptive software plus scheduled after-school sessions.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> +0.37 SD in maths and +0.23 SD in Hindi in 4.5 months, from <b>pre-language-model</b> adaptive technology. Similar absolute gains for all, larger relative gains for weaker students. This is the benchmark the generative generation has not demonstrably beaten.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> anything about generative AI — there is no language model here. That is precisely its role in this talk.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Muralidharan, K., Singh, A., &amp; Ganimian, A. J. (2019). <i>American Economic Review</i>, 109(4), 1426–1460.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the published article</span></div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A4 — Backup · Rori (Ghana)\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · low-bandwidth access</p>\n  <h2>“The impact of Rori, an AI powered conversational math tutor accessible via WhatsApp, on the math performance of approximately 1,000 students in grades 3–9 across 11 schools in Ghana.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> control continued regular maths instruction; treatment used Rori for two 30-minute sessions a week over 8 months <b>in addition to</b> regular instruction — extra time again.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> d = 0.36–0.37, on basic phones over low-bandwidth networks. The access story matters for low-resource settings.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> treating it as confirmatory. Preprint; assignment at school level with only 11 units; added instructional time uncontrolled. The authors' own caveat: “the results should be interpreted judiciously, as they only report on year 1.”</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Henkel, O., Horne-Robinson, H., Kozhakhmetova, N., &amp; Lee, A. (2024). arXiv:2402.09809. Preprint.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) · preprint, treat as preliminary</span></div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A5 — Backup · ASSISTments\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · the other pre-LLM benchmark</p>\n  <h2>“Do students in schools that use ASSISTments for mathematics homework learn more than students in schools who do homework without ASSISTments?”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> schools randomised to ASSISTments — online homework with immediate feedback <b>plus teacher training</b> — against business-as-usual homework practices.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> g = 0.18 at state scale over a full school year; 2,850 seventh-graders, 43 schools, Maine. Students with low prior achievement benefited most.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> claims about chatbots — there is no language model. And the effect includes the teacher professional development, not just the software.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Roschelle, J., Feng, M., Murphy, R. F., &amp; Mason, C. A. (2016). <i>AERA Open</i>, 2(4), 1–12.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Verified in T-006 (fable) against the published article</span></div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A6 — Backup · Tutor CoPilot\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · AI aimed at the tutor, not the student</p>\n  <h2>“Training novice educators with expert guidance is important for effectiveness but expensive … We introduce Tutor CoPilot, a novel Human-AI approach that leverages a model of expert thinking to provide expert-like guidance to tutors as they tutor.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> tutors randomised to access or no access. <b>The model never talks to the student.</b> Preregistered; 900 tutors, ~1,800 students in Title I schools, grades 3–8.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> +4 percentage points on the platform's exit ticket (62% → 66%), and most of the benefit goes to the lowest-rated tutors. A closed menu of seven human-chosen strategies is a workable division of labour.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> learning gains in general: the distal standardised test was null. The significant effect is on the platform's own progression gate.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">Wang, R. E., Ribeiro, A. T., Robinson, C. D., Loeb, S., &amp; Demszky, D. (2024). arXiv:2410.03017.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Researched in T-002 (claude) · question quoted by fable in T-006</span></div>\n-->\n\n## SLIDE A7 — Backup · Eedi / LearnLM\n\n<!--visual\n<div class=\"card\">\n  <p class=\"kicker\">Study card · supervised, not autonomous</p>\n  <h2>“To evaluate whether generative AI might help expand access to [one-to-one tutoring], we conducted an exploratory randomized controlled trial with N = 165 students across five UK secondary schools.”</h2>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>The comparison:</b> two levels. Students randomised to static hints vs live 1:1 chat; within chat, sessions randomised to a human tutor alone vs <b>LearnLM drafting every message under human supervision</b> — the tutor revised each draft until they would send it themselves.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Licenses:</b> supervised AI matched human tutors on remediation (93.0% vs 91.2%, overlapping intervals), tutors approved 76.4% of drafts with zero or minimal edits, and the abstract reports +5.5 points on novel problems.</p>\n  <p class=\"qline\"><b>Does not license:</b> autonomous AI tutoring — a human vetted every single message. Nor anything confirmatory: the authors call it exploratory, N=165, and the author list includes the vendor and the platform.</p>\n  <p class=\"cite\">LearnLM Team, Google, &amp; Eedi (2025). arXiv:2512.23633. Exploratory.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"provbar\"><span class=\"prov prov-e\">Evidence <span class=\"src\">· question quoted from the source</span></span> <span class=\"chain\">Researched in T-002 (claude) · question quoted by fable in T-006</span></div>\n-->\n","numLines":1105,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1105}}}
